{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9KYM6XhGtUrijNyFrv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W2WVV6mPpbyfepuCaFRxoB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6752,"cache_read_input_tokens":25496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6752},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"eb4c38c3-ff38-4580-9c20-154b1950fce4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:58.015Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9KY5UNQDG1uGXNoffi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01W2WVV6mPpbyfepuCaFRxoB":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W2WVV6mPpbyfepuCaFRxoB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# my-3dge\n2\t\n3\tA fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI coding agents. Everything it draws, animates and plays is\n4\tmade by code or readable data. It renders with three.js on WebGPU only, and simulates with Rapier physics. Gameplay runs\n5\tonly on the CPU: it is reproducible, runs headless in Node, and the GPU improves speed and looks, never play. Game UI is\n6\tHTML/CSS. Games see a small public API built on the most widely used web tools (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright,\n7\tESLint, Prettier), so any agent can work with what it already knows; behind that API, the engine's internals use the\n8\tbest approach its builders can execute well.\n9\t\n10\t## Make a game with my-3dge\n11\t\n12\t**Lean, the default, for any agent (a chat in a browser too):** read [`lean/README.md`](lean/README.md), one file\n13\t(under 20,000 tokens) holding a small game that already plays, every look, filter and built-in id, and the whole\n14\tAPI. A game is\n15\tone HTML page that loads the engine from a CDN, the way three.js or Rapier load: nothing to install, build or test.\n16\tOpen the page in a browser with WebGPU. To hand it to an agent, give it the raw file,\n17\thttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md (docs/HANDOFF.md has the prompt).\n18\t\n19\t**The full workspace, for a coding agent that also wants the engine's checks** (headless tests, replays, renders read\n20\tas numbers): a game lives in a repository of its own, never in this one. In the empty folder or fresh repository where\n21\tthe game should live, run:\n22\t\n23\t```sh\n24\tsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" .\n25\t```\n26\t\n27\tThat makes the game there (named after the folder; `--name dragon-run` names it): the engine, read-only and pinned\n28\tby `engine-lock.json`; a small starter game in `labs/game/` that already plays, is won by its bot and passes its\n29\ttests; and an `AGENTS.md` written for making a game. Then follow that `AGENTS.md`. Without git, unzip\n30\t[the ZIP of main](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip) and run\n31\t`node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`. For the owner: [`docs/HANDOFF.md`](docs/HANDOFF.md).\n32\t\n33\t## Status\n34\t\n35\t[`docs/PROGRESS.md`](docs/PROGRESS.md) is the one-screen status, rewritten at each gate: where things\n36\tstand, what is next and the open issues. The ledger in [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md) §14 has every work package.\n37\t\n38\t## The doctrine\n39\t\n40\t[`DOCTRINE.md`](DOCTRINE.md) is the owner's doctrine for this engine, and it governs everything else: agent-readable,\n41\tagent-operable, verifiable without a display, agent-accessible assets, reproducible on the development platform, WebGPU\n42\tonly, common ground, quality under the hood, mastery over novelty, and discovery first. [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) turns\n43\tit into the rules every change follows; [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md) §3 lists the check behind each principle.\n44\t\n45\t## The Stress Box\n46\t\n47\tThe engine's integration target is the *Stress test: 3D world* of\n48\t[my-3d2dge](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), rebuilt from the ground up in six stages, from a small room\n49\tto a hall with a crowd of 5,000 (`PLAN.md` §9.0). Each stage adds engine systems, not game content, and ends with a box\n50\tthat runs, is measured and is proved. The game is the Playground (below); games that agents make live in repositories\n51\tof their own.\n52\t\n53\t## The Playground\n54\t\n55\tThe game and tech demo (Phase 13, [`docs/PLAYGROUND.md`](docs/PLAYGROUND.md)): one block of everything the engine\n56\tdoes, a house, a street, a gym, a hill, a range and a ring, where the player moves as in Apex Legends, in five cameras\n57\tand three looks, with doors, melee, guns, neighbours, a dog, sound, day and night. Open\n58\t[`playground.html`](playground.html) (below), or run `npm run dev` and open http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/playground/: a\n59\tsplash screen, then the start menu (Play, Go to, Settings, Controls, Photo mode, Watch the tour, Owner checks, Tools);\n60\tH in play lists every control. [`labs/playground/README.md`](labs/playground/README.md) says how it plays and what\n61\teach scene shows.\n62\t\n63\t## How to start\n64\t\n65\t```sh\n66\tbash scripts/setup.sh           # Node 24.22.2 if missing (SHA-256 checked), the read-only sources, then npm ci\n67\tnpm run typecheck               # tsc -b over tsconfig.check.json's projects (incremental), on the pinned types\n68\tnpm test                        # vitest run\n69\tnode x ci --local               # the merge gate: every tier, summary in out/ci/summary.md\n70\t```\n71\t\n72\t`scripts/setup.sh` is idempotent and prints the `export` lines for `PATH`, `MY3D2DGE_SRC` and `SHARDFALL_SRC` (it\n73\tappends them to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` in Claude Code). The development platform is Linux x64 with Node 24.22.2 and\n74\tChromium 153 (WebGPU on SwiftShader). The rest of the toolchain is `npm run check` (types, lint, format and the\n75\trepository checks), `npm run e2e` (the browser suites) and the `node x` command line (`node x help`).\n76\t\n77\t## The one-file playground\n78\t\n79\t[`playground.html`](playground.html) is the playground as one self-contained HTML page: its script, styles and\n80\tWebAssembly inline, nothing fetched. Download it and open it in a browser with WebGPU (a desktop Chrome or Edge); it is\n81\thow the engine is distributed and tested ([ADR-0023](docs/decisions/ADR-0023-owner-calls-2026-10-15.md) amendment 5,\n82\tin place of the Vercel deployment). Only the playground is in it: the labs index and the Stress Box need the dev\n83\tserver (`npm run dev`). It is rebuilt from the sources with every change that alters it, and the merge\n84\tgate fails while it is stale:\n85\t\n86\t```sh\n87\tnode x html --write             # rebuild playground.html (about 11 s, the same bytes every time); commit it\n88\tnode x html --check             # the gate's check: the committed file matches a build of its sources\n89\t```\n90\t\n91\t`npm run build && npx vite preview` still builds and serves every page (`dist/`) locally.\n92\t\n93\t## Where things are\n94\t\n95\t- [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md): the rules for every change; [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) adds Claude Code's notes.\n96\t- [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md): the full plan. §1–§3: the summary, the goals, and the doctrine turned into checks; §9: the\n97\t  work packages, stage by stage; §14: the status ledger.\n98\t- [`docs/decisions/`](docs/decisions/): the ADRs (PLAN.md §13).\n99\t- [`docs/research/`](docs/research/): the eight studies of my-3d2dge (October 2026) the plan is based on.\n100\t\n101\t[MIT License](LICENSE).\n102\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"08849f9f-158f-441e-8263-64d20094676d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:58.227Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/README.md","content":"# my-3dge\n\nA fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI coding agents. Everything it draws, animates and plays is\nmade by code or readable data. It renders with three.js on WebGPU only, and simulates with Rapier physics. Gameplay runs\nonly on the CPU: it is reproducible, runs headless in Node, and the GPU improves speed and looks, never play. Game UI is\nHTML/CSS. Games see a small public API built on the most widely used web tools (TypeScript, Vite, Vitest, Playwright,\nESLint, Prettier), so any agent can work with what it already knows; behind that API, the engine's internals use the\nbest approach its builders can execute well.\n\n## Make a game with my-3dge\n\n**Lean, the default, for any agent (a chat in a browser too):** read [`lean/README.md`](lean/README.md), one file\n(under 20,000 tokens) holding a small game that already plays, every look, filter and built-in id, and the whole\nAPI. A game is\none HTML page that loads the engine from a CDN, the way three.js or Rapier load: nothing to install, build or test.\nOpen the page in a browser with WebGPU. To hand it to an agent, give it the raw file,\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/main/lean/README.md (docs/HANDOFF.md has the prompt).\n\n**The full workspace, for a coding agent that also wants the engine's checks** (headless tests, replays, renders read\nas numbers): a game lives in a repository of its own, never in this one. In the empty folder or fresh repository where\nthe game should live, run:\n\n```sh\nsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" .\n```\n\nThat makes the game there (named after the folder; `--name dragon-run` names it): the engine, read-only and pinned\nby `engine-lock.json`; a small starter game in `labs/game/` that already plays, is won by its bot and passes its\ntests; and an `AGENTS.md` written for making a game. Then follow that `AGENTS.md`. Without git, unzip\n[the ZIP of main](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge/archive/refs/heads/main.zip) and run\n`node <unzipped folder>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir>`. For the owner: [`docs/HANDOFF.md`](docs/HANDOFF.md).\n\n## Status\n\n[`docs/PROGRESS.md`](docs/PROGRESS.md) is the one-screen status, rewritten at each gate: where things\nstand, what is next and the open issues. The ledger in [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md) §14 has every work package.\n\n## The doctrine\n\n[`DOCTRINE.md`](DOCTRINE.md) is the owner's doctrine for this engine, and it governs everything else: agent-readable,\nagent-operable, verifiable without a display, agent-accessible assets, reproducible on the development platform, WebGPU\nonly, common ground, quality under the hood, mastery over novelty, and discovery first. [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) turns\nit into the rules every change follows; [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md) §3 lists the check behind each principle.\n\n## The Stress Box\n\nThe engine's integration target is the *Stress test: 3D world* of\n[my-3d2dge](https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), rebuilt from the ground up in six stages, from a small room\nto a hall with a crowd of 5,000 (`PLAN.md` §9.0). Each stage adds engine systems, not game content, and ends with a box\nthat runs, is measured and is proved. The game is the Playground (below); games that agents make live in repositories\nof their own.\n\n## The Playground\n\nThe game and tech demo (Phase 13, [`docs/PLAYGROUND.md`](docs/PLAYGROUND.md)): one block of everything the engine\ndoes, a house, a street, a gym, a hill, a range and a ring, where the player moves as in Apex Legends, in five cameras\nand three looks, with doors, melee, guns, neighbours, a dog, sound, day and night. Open\n[`playground.html`](playground.html) (below), or run `npm run dev` and open http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/playground/: a\nsplash screen, then the start menu (Play, Go to, Settings, Controls, Photo mode, Watch the tour, Owner checks, Tools);\nH in play lists every control. [`labs/playground/README.md`](labs/playground/README.md) says how it plays and what\neach scene shows.\n\n## How to start\n\n```sh\nbash scripts/setup.sh           # Node 24.22.2 if missing (SHA-256 checked), the read-only sources, then npm ci\nnpm run typecheck               # tsc -b over tsconfig.check.json's projects (incremental), on the pinned types\nnpm test                        # vitest run\nnode x ci --local               # the merge gate: every tier, summary in out/ci/summary.md\n```\n\n`scripts/setup.sh` is idempotent and prints the `export` lines for `PATH`, `MY3D2DGE_SRC` and `SHARDFALL_SRC` (it\nappends them to `$CLAUDE_ENV_FILE` in Claude Code). The development platform is Linux x64 with Node 24.22.2 and\nChromium 153 (WebGPU on SwiftShader). The rest of the toolchain is `npm run check` (types, lint, format and the\nrepository checks), `npm run e2e` (the browser suites) and the `node x` command line (`node x help`).\n\n## The one-file playground\n\n[`playground.html`](playground.html) is the playground as one self-contained HTML page: its script, styles and\nWebAssembly inline, nothing fetched. Download it and open it in a browser with WebGPU (a desktop Chrome or Edge); it is\nhow the engine is distributed and tested ([ADR-0023](docs/decisions/ADR-0023-owner-calls-2026-10-15.md) amendment 5,\nin place of the Vercel deployment). Only the playground is in it: the labs index and the Stress Box need the dev\nserver (`npm run dev`). It is rebuilt from the sources with every change that alters it, and the merge\ngate fails while it is stale:\n\n```sh\nnode x html --write             # rebuild playground.html (about 11 s, the same bytes every time); commit it\nnode x html --check             # the gate's check: the committed file matches a build of its sources\n```\n\n`npm run build && npx vite preview` still builds and serves every page (`dist/`) locally.\n\n## Where things are\n\n- [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md): the rules for every change; [`CLAUDE.md`](CLAUDE.md) adds Claude Code's notes.\n- [`PLAN.md`](PLAN.md): the full plan. §1–§3: the summary, the goals, and the doctrine turned into checks; §9: the\n  work packages, stage by stage; §14: the status ledger.\n- [`docs/decisions/`](docs/decisions/): the ADRs (PLAN.md §13).\n- [`docs/research/`](docs/research/): the eight studies of my-3d2dge (October 2026) the plan is based on.\n\n[MIT License](LICENSE).\n","numLines":102,"startLine":1,"totalLines":102}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01W2WVV6mPpbyfepuCaFRxoB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"system","subtype":"commands_changed","commands":[{"name":"deep-research","description":"Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. (dynamic workflow)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design","description":"Grant or revoke Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"consent | revoke","builtin":true},{"name":"design-sync","description":"Push a React design system to claude.ai/design. This runs a converter that bundles the real component code (from Storybook or a bare package) and uploads it. Use when the user runs /design-sync or says \"sync my design system to Claude Design\".","argumentHint":"[<project hint, e.g. \"Acme DS\">]","builtin":true},{"name":"dataviz","description":"Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"update-config","description":"Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"verify","description":"Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"debug","description":"Enable debug logging for this session and help diagnose issues","argumentHint":"[issue description]","builtin":true},{"name":"code-review","description":"Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's recipe covers them) at the effort level given first, before any target (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings to many, some uncertain; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply them to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.","argumentHint":"[low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra] [--fix] [--comment] [<pr#>|<branch>|<path>] [--max-findings <n>|all]","aliases":["review"],"builtin":true},{"name":"simplify","description":"Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.","argumentHint":"[<target>]","builtin":true},{"name":"batch","description":"Research and plan a large-scale change, then execute it in parallel across 5–30 isolated worktree agents that each open a PR.","argumentHint":"<instruction>","builtin":true},{"name":"fewer-permission-prompts","description":"Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"doctor","description":"Health-check the user's Claude Code setup and fix issues: diagnose installation health — what the `claude doctor` terminal diagnostics cover — from local data (duplicate or leftover installs, PATH, unparseable settings files, broken or colliding agent definitions, skills whose frontmatter fails to parse); find unused skills, MCP servers, and plugins versus their context cost and disable dead weight; deduplicate local CLAUDE.md files against checked-in ones; trim checked-in CLAUDE.md files by cutting content a session could derive from the codebase (directory layouts, tech-stack lists, architecture overviews) while keeping gotchas, rationale, and non-standard conventions; migrate always-loaded CLAUDE.md guidance into lazy skills and nested CLAUDE.md files; flag slow hooks and context-heavy extensions; check the installed version is current; make auto mode the default permission mode; and pre-approve frequently denied read-only commands. Use when the user asks for a doctor run, checkup, audit, tune-up, or cleanup of their Claude Code setup or configuration.","argumentHint":"[prompt-audit [<path>]]","aliases":["checkup"],"builtin":true},{"name":"loop","description":"Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace.","argumentHint":"[interval] [prompt]","aliases":["proactive"],"builtin":true},{"name":"add-entry","description":"Adding an entry of a kind through node x new and its template in tools/templates/ — a module, a box scene, a prop, material, texture, geometry, look, emitter, telegraph, thing, weapon, interactable (what a player uses or strikes), level written as storeys, movement profile, camera mode, build, body, move, action, procedural rig, sfx, song, a playground feel course or a rendering feature — which kind, the command, what its test proves, wiring it in, seeing it, finishing; and adding the template when a kind is written by hand a third time. Use whenever you add a new entry of any kind, or catch yourself writing the third entry of a kind that has no template. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"animation-authoring","description":"Authoring animation in my-3dge — poses as effector targets in metres (B(forward, right, up)) solved by IK onto the humanoid's rotation skeleton, builds and bodies, moves (strikes as data), actions (multi-beat sequences), library clips (find, show, play), procedural rigs, reactions and secondary motion — and checking it by numbers (animCheck, animCheckBody, actionCheck, rigCheck, node x qa anim, node x sheet, the gallery, the Animation Lab). Use when you add or change a build, body, move, action, clip, pose, rig or reaction, touch engine/anim, or an anim QA metric fails. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"determinism-debugging","description":"How to find and fix a determinism bug in my-3dge — a replay that misses its golden hashes, Node and Chromium giving different hashes, two runs of the same replay disagreeing, or a capture that does not continue exactly. Bisect with node x replay --bisect to the first step, entity and field, then check the usual causes (Math aliases, **, iteration order, view settings, async inside withSimMath, module state, presentation leaking into the sim). Use whenever x replay, a replay test or a hash comparison fails. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"escalation","description":"When and how to escalate to the owner in my-3dge — the cases that need the owner's approval (binary assets, new dependencies, repository settings, spending, a principle that cannot be met, going against the doctrine), the 15-minute rule, and the node x esc commands that record every escalation and every call made without an answer. Use before any such action, and when the SessionStart hook or the Stop hook names an open escalation. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"perf-budgets","description":"Performance in my-3dge as counters against budgets, never frames per second — the budget files under tests/baselines/perf/ (Node steps, hash and capture; the crowd ladder at 100, 1,000 and 5,000 agents; a page's times, draw calls, triangles, textures, geometries and pipelines, 0 after the warm-up), the content budgets in the data (a geometry's vertices, the effects' pools, the light pool, a body's draw calls, a level's per storey), node x perf and __engine.stats(), and how to meet them (the warm-up, instancing, the light pool, features and quality tiers, what the hash reads). Use when you add or change something drawn or simulated at scale, when node x perf --budget or a QA budget fails, or before recording a budget. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"rapier","description":"Rapier physics in my-3dge under the reproducibility contract — bodies, queries, stepping, the hash and captures through engine/physics (the only module that imports Rapier), the collision groups and pinned parameters, and the traps measured on Rapier 0.21 (timeOfImpact vs time_of_impact vs toi, castShape's targetDistance, queries before the first step, handles as float64 bits, the controller's across-then-down move, autostep climbing about 5 cm high). Use when adding bodies, colliders or queries, touching engine/physics, or debugging a physics hash or capture. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"stress-box","description":"The Stress Box (labs/box), the engine's integration target - its layout, how to add a scene or a variant, and the quick loop (x set, x sim, x shot, x replay --browser page, x inspect). Use when you work on the box, add or tune a box scene, or need to show an engine capability in the box. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"three-webgpu","description":"Writing three.js code in my-3dge — the pinned release's idioms on WebGPU only (three/webgpu, three/tsl, three/addons), what game code meets (material parameters, the math classes) versus the TSL, compute and node post-processing that stay inside engine/gfx, the banned APIs and their replacements, and the traps from newer or older releases. Use before writing, reviewing or debugging code that imports three.js. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"visual-qa","description":"Checking what my-3dge draws without looking — the look metrics (coverage, luma spread, dark and blown-out shares, palette, flat share, dithering, edges, the largest object, the protagonist), the ID pass (which object covers which pixels, and why an unseen one is unseen), the 48 × 27 text thumbnails and their baselines, contact sheets (x film, x gallery, x sheet), film comparisons that name the objects whose pixels changed, the agent eye in Node, and when to hand an image to the visual-reviewer subagent. Use when you change rendering, a look, a camera, a material or a scene's view, when a look note or a thumbnail check fails, or when a question is whether something shows. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"x-loop","description":"The my-3dge work loop, edit → check → test → report. Which command to run after a change (npm run check, npm test, npm run e2e, node x …), how to read the report.json files instead of scrollback, and how to finish a work package (docs, ledger, commit). Use whenever you change code in this repository, or are unsure what to run next. (from .scratch/engine/.claude/skills — applies when working on files under .scratch/engine/) (project)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"schedule","description":"Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["routines"],"builtin":true},{"name":"claude-api","description":"Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-authoring","description":"Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run","description":"Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run-skill-generator","description":"Author or improve the run-<unit> skill - a per-project skill that tells agents how to build, launch, and drive this project's app. Use when the user asks to set up the project, get it running, write run instructions, or verify build/run steps work from a clean environment.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"plugin-authoring","description":"Make a mod: a change to Claude's own interface or behaviour, such as a panel or pane that stays beside the chat and updates, a band, status line, toast, slash command, or hook on tool calls. Use to make, change or debug a mod or its hooks module.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"advisor","description":"Let Claude consult a stronger model at key moments","argumentHint":"[fable|opus|sonnet|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"agents","description":"(removed) Ask Claude to create/manage subagents, or edit .claude/agents/","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"auto-mode-setup","description":"Teach auto mode about your environment, plus optional rule tweaks","argumentHint":"[--request-id <uuid>] (--wizard posture=… scope=… depth=… --propose | --expect-sha256 <64-hex> --apply-file <path>)","builtin":true},{"name":"autocompact","description":"Configure the auto-compact window size","argumentHint":"[auto|<tokens>]","builtin":true},{"name":"clear","description":"Start a new session with empty context; previous session stays on disk (resumable with /resume)","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["reset","new"],"builtin":true},{"name":"color","description":"Set the prompt bar color for this session","argumentHint":"[red|blue|green|yellow|purple|orange|pink|cyan|default]","builtin":true},{"name":"compact","description":"Free up context by summarizing the conversation so far","argumentHint":"<optional custom summarization instructions>","builtin":true},{"name":"config","description":"Set a setting by key","argumentHint":"key=value","aliases":["settings"],"builtin":true},{"name":"output-style","description":"List output styles or switch to one","argumentHint":"[style]","builtin":true},{"name":"context","description":"Show current context usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"effort","description":"Set effort level for model usage","argumentHint":"<low|medium|high|xhigh|max|auto|ultracode [on|off]>","builtin":true},{"name":"fast","description":"Toggle fast mode (Opus 5.5)","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"focus","description":"Toggle focus view: just your prompt, summary, and response","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"heapdump","description":"Dump the JS heap to the Desktop; on Linux with no Desktop folder, the home directory","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"init","description":"Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"mcp","description":"Manage MCP servers","argumentHint":"[reconnect|enable|disable [<server>|all]]","builtin":true},{"name":"import","description":"Import config from another AI coding agent","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"model","description":"Set the AI model for Claude Code","argumentHint":"<model>","builtin":true},{"name":"__remote-workflow","description":"Run the workflow script delivered in this session environment (server-launched sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-launch-exec","description":"Execute a server-launched workflow handoff (workflow_launch event sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-plugins","description":"Activate pending plugin changes in the current session","argumentHint":"[--force]","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-skills","description":"Pick up skills added or changed on disk during this session","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"rename","description":"Rename the current conversation","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["name"],"builtin":true},{"name":"ultrareview","description":"Start a cloud agent that finds and verifies bugs in your branch (~5-15 min, $5-$25 USD) · Runs in a cloud session. See https://code.claude.com/docs/en/claude-code-on-the-web","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"security-review","description":"Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage-credits","description":"Configure usage credits or request them from your admin when you hit a limit","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"extra-usage","description":"Renamed to /usage-credits","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage","description":"Show session cost, plan usage, and what's contributing to your limits","argumentHint":"","aliases":["cost","stats"],"builtin":true},{"name":"insights","description":"Generate a report analyzing your Claude Code sessions","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"recap","description":"Generate a one-line session recap now","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"skill-doctor","description":"Show which loaded skills are unused and costing context","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"goal","description":"Set a goal — keep working until the condition is met","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-consent","description":"Grant Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-revoke","description":"Revoke Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"list-agents","description":"List subagents, teammates, and other Claude sessions you can message","argumentHint":"","aliases":["peers"],"builtin":true},{"name":"team-onboarding","description":"Help teammates ramp on Claude Code with a guide from your usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"setup-writing-style","description":"Learns how the user writes from their own sent messages and docs, and builds a voice profile so future drafts sound like them instead of generic AI. The profile is saved as the my-writing-style skill. Use when the user asks to set up, learn, or capture their writing voice, or complains that drafts sound generic or unlike them and no my-writing-style profile exists. Only for drafting text the user will send as themselves, not for Claude's own replies. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:setup-writing-style"]}],"uuid":"09bb254a-4fa2-4215-98c0-dc90fb7e8252","session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9KYM6XhGtUrijNyFrv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0186UDE1uLSSETeeZPkE8X3B","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/AGENTS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6752,"cache_read_input_tokens":25496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6752},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"93a17b7b-c879-45ce-8fc5-790f32fbaa83","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:58.536Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9KY5UNQDG1uGXNoffi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0186UDE1uLSSETeeZPkE8X3B":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/AGENTS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0186UDE1uLSSETeeZPkE8X3B","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md: rules for every change to my-3dge\n2\t\n3\tmy-3dge is a fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI agents. DOCTRINE.md governs everything here:\n4\tread it first. The plan and its ledger are PLAN.md; generated docs start at docs/INDEX.md. Making a game? Not here:\n5\tread lean/README.md, or README.md's first section for a repository of its own.\n6\t\n7\t## How to work\n8\tWhere the doctrine and the plan are silent, use your judgment and record the call. When going against the doctrine\n9\tlooks best, escalate with your reasoning (below); never deviate quietly. On-demand work packages wait for their\n10\tmeasured trigger (PLAN.md §9.1); the playground (Phase 13, ADR-0025) is what fires them now.\n11\tTesting budget (ADR-0022): verify with cheap, objective checks (suites, hashes, counts), never wall-clock time on\n12\tthis shared machine. Long tests never hold up work (ADR-0023): the full run goes on GitHub after each merge, nobody\n13\twaiting on it. Unclear after a cheap test means no proven benefit: stop, record it as an open issue starting \"Owner:\"\n14\tand move on.\n15\t\n16\t## Setup\n17\tbash scripts/setup.sh (idempotent; the SessionStart hook runs it) installs Node 24.22.2 (.nvmrc), resolves the\n18\tread-only sources, $MY3D2DGE_SRC (my-3d2dge@e37e4ee) and $SHARDFALL_SRC (shardfall@fa2dab6), cloning them into\n19\t.cache/src-* when unset, and runs npm ci. Never edit the sources. Offline it keeps Node 22: everything runs, but Node\n20\tgoldens are recorded on 24. npm's warning that an .npmrc allow-scripts setting is ignored is expected.\n21\t\n22\t## The doctrine, as checks\n23\t1. Agent-readable: one concept per file, ≤ 400 lines (hard 600), a JSDoc file comment; docs come from comments.\n24\t   → npm run check, x docs --check\n25\t2. Agent-operable: every capability through npm scripts, node x or __engine, never only in a page.\n26\t   → command smoke tests, the help() check\n27\t3. Verifiable without a display: the sim runs headless; visual checks are numbers (ID pass, metrics, thumbnails);\n28\t   the HTML/CSS UI is checked through DOM queries. → npm test, x replay, npm run e2e\n29\t4. Assets: procedural first, then text and data; binary only with the owner's approval (fonts are pre-approved).\n30\t   → x check, x esc\n31\t5. Reproducible: fixed 60 Hz, recorded intents, named seeded RNG. Game code writes the standard Math; the sim swaps\n32\t   in fdlibm ports while it steps, so one golden per replay holds in Node and Chromium.\n33\t   → ESLint sim bans, replays, the drift test\n34\t6. WebGPU only, no WebGL fallback. Gameplay runs only on the CPU: sim-side code never imports gfx, the renderer, the\n35\t   DOM or Web Audio, and no GPU result reaches the sim. → ESLint layer rules, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts\n36\t7. Common ground: game code meets npm, Vite, Vitest, Playwright and a public API in plain terms; a bespoke\n37\t   game-facing tool only where nothing fits, with the reason in its file comment. → verifier, ADR-0019\n38\t8. Quality under the hood: game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts; behind them, the best\n39\t   approach you can execute well. Every public export is documented and every error names its fix.\n40\t   → ESLint public-API rule, x docs --check, ADR-0020\n41\t9. Mastery: only the pins tools/deps.json records, and an upgrade is a work package of its own; write the pinned\n42\t   release's idioms (docs/THREE-DELTA.md). → x deps --check, banned names\n43\t10. Discovery first: target this container (Node 24, Chromium 153, WebGPU on SwiftShader); no production hardening\n44\t    outside Phase H, except ADR-0021's touch controls and the one-file playground (ADR-0023 amendment 5).\n45\t\n46\t## Escalate, don't stall\n47\tOwner approval needed (a binary asset, a new dependency, spending money, a repo setting, any other outward action;\n48\tpushes, stage PRs, merges and branch deletions are pre-approved, ADR-0021), a principle can't be met, or going\n49\tagainst the doctrine looks best: node x esc open … → post it → wait up to 15 minutes, doing the preferred\n50\talternative, never the action itself → answered: act on it. No answer: commit, decide, node x esc decide …,\n51\tcontinue, and report later conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, conflict and call.\n52\tThe owner answers in the game: each escalation awaiting their review gets a decide check, in plain words with its\n53\tchoices, in labs/playground/ui/checkList.ts (gone once answered), and whatever only they can judge (feel, looks,\n54\tsound, devices) a try check there, never a checklist in prose alone; their pasted answers come back to record.\n55\t\n56\t## The loop\n57\t1. node x deps --update: adopt compatible releases first, in a commit of their own.\n58\t2. Read your WP in PLAN.md §9 and only the sections it cites, and the file comments of the modules you touch (not\n59\t   whole files). The sources being ported are read-only (node x src).\n60\t3. Edit. The after-edit hook formats and lints the file: fix what it reports.\n61\t4. npm run check → npm test. Browser specs (npm run e2e -- <spec>) only for what only they check that you touched\n62\t   (rendering, the page, the DOM) or a failure you are finding and fixing, never the whole set while you work.\n63\t5. Read out/**/report.json. Open images only when a metric points at one (use the visual-reviewer subagent).\n64\t6. Show it in the box or a fixture test. Update the ledger row in PLAN.md §14 (in ultracode lanes, report it).\n65\t7. Commit \"<area>: <what> (WP-x.y)\". Report the commit, the report paths and any escalations.\n66\t\n67\t## Rules\n68\t- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Gameplay time is in 60 Hz sim ticks. Randomness comes from named streams.\n69\t- DOCTRINE.md changes only when the owner asks. Never edit out/ or node_modules/ by hand.\n70\t- Never skip, disable or loosen a test to get green. Baselines change only with a written reason.\n71\t- Merge on the quick gate (ADR-0023): squash-merge a PR once node x ci --local is green on its head. GitHub then\n72\t  runs the full run on main; nobody waits on it. node x ci --status (the SessionStart hook prints it) says how\n73\t  main's last one went: a red main is fixed before other work.\n74\t- At each gate and at the end of a goal: push, open the stage's PR, merge it, then wait for main's full run and\n75\t  report it. Start the next stage from the updated main (PLAN.md §11.1).\n76\t- No deployment: the engine is distributed and tested as playground.html, the playground in one self-contained page\n77\t  (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of 2026-11-02; Vercel is gone). A change that alters it commits it rebuilt\n78\t  (node x html --write); the quick gate fails while it is stale (x html --check). The touch controls have no tests\n79\t  (ADR-0021); fix what the owner reports from the file.\n80\t- Never downgrade silently: every downgrade emits an advice code.\n81\t- Ask the entry, never the id: shared code reads registry hooks and never compares ids.\n82\t- No game content in the engine; the box's own content stays in labs/box/, the playground's in labs/playground/\n83\t  (PLAN.md §5.7).\n84\t- Docs are generated from comments: never restate a fact in prose (x docs --check).\n85\t- Deviations from PLAN.md become ADR amendments in docs/decisions/, with the plan edited in the same commit (§11.4).\n86\t- Blocked by the core? Write the smallest core change as a proposal in your WP notes. Don't widen your WP.\n87\t- Say plainly what you did not verify.\n88\t\n89\t## Done means\n90\tVerify commands green; npm run check and npm test green; the specs for what only T2 checks that you touched green;\n91\tshown in the box or a fixture; file comments and docs current; a template for any kind written by hand a third time;\n92\tQA for kinds with a QA family; a verifier review for L-size WPs; no new advice codes; escalations recorded; ledger\n93\tupdated.\n94\t\n95\t## Where things are\n96\tTask guides (docs/INDEX.md opens with them), each read when the task needs it:\n97\t- docs/PROGRESS.md: read first, every session: where things stand, what is next, the open issues.\n98\t- docs/TESTING.md: the tiers, and which unit tests and specs a change selects.\n99\t- docs/PLAYGROUND.md: the playground (Phase 13): the site, the move set and its reference values, the five cameras,\n100\t  the three looks, and how feel and looks are checked.\n101\t- docs/HANDOFF.md: handing the engine to game agents (the prompt, many games at once), starting in a new environment,\n102\t  and the box against my-3d2dge's stress-world.\n103\t- lean/: the default handoff, the engine as a library (ADR-0024 amendment 1); docs/GAME-GUIDE.md and labs/game/, the\n104\t  full workspace's guide and starter. Keep lean/README.md, lean/game.html, the guide and the starter true when a\n105\t  public API changes (`x docs --write` rebuilds lean/my3dge.js and writes the generated blocks).\n106\t  tools/game/new-game.mjs makes a game; `node x engine --test` proves one passes its own gate.\n107\t- labs/box/README.md: the box's scenes, settings and quick loop; adding a scene or a variant (a variant is a new\n108\t  scene file or a settings preset, never a change of meaning in a passing scene).\n109\t- tests/replays/README.md: the replay format; checking, recording and making replays.\n110\t- tools/templates/README.md: the kinds `node x new <kind> <id>` writes from a template and checks (a module, scene,\n111\t  prop, weapon, level…), where each goes, what shows it, and how to add a kind (`node x new` lists them).\n112\t- .claude/skills/: the skills, each loaded when its description says (x-loop after any change, add-entry for a new\n113\t  entry of a kind, stress-box for the box, determinism-debugging when hashes part, animation-authoring, visual-qa,\n114\t  perf-budgets, rapier, three-webgpu, escalation).\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"6a5cf8b3-8e4f-44e9-9bdf-ac969c6064a4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:58.591Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md: rules for every change to my-3dge\n\nmy-3dge is a fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI agents. DOCTRINE.md governs everything here:\nread it first. The plan and its ledger are PLAN.md; generated docs start at docs/INDEX.md. Making a game? Not here:\nread lean/README.md, or README.md's first section for a repository of its own.\n\n## How to work\nWhere the doctrine and the plan are silent, use your judgment and record the call. When going against the doctrine\nlooks best, escalate with your reasoning (below); never deviate quietly. On-demand work packages wait for their\nmeasured trigger (PLAN.md §9.1); the playground (Phase 13, ADR-0025) is what fires them now.\nTesting budget (ADR-0022): verify with cheap, objective checks (suites, hashes, counts), never wall-clock time on\nthis shared machine. Long tests never hold up work (ADR-0023): the full run goes on GitHub after each merge, nobody\nwaiting on it. Unclear after a cheap test means no proven benefit: stop, record it as an open issue starting \"Owner:\"\nand move on.\n\n## Setup\nbash scripts/setup.sh (idempotent; the SessionStart hook runs it) installs Node 24.22.2 (.nvmrc), resolves the\nread-only sources, $MY3D2DGE_SRC (my-3d2dge@e37e4ee) and $SHARDFALL_SRC (shardfall@fa2dab6), cloning them into\n.cache/src-* when unset, and runs npm ci. Never edit the sources. Offline it keeps Node 22: everything runs, but Node\ngoldens are recorded on 24. npm's warning that an .npmrc allow-scripts setting is ignored is expected.\n\n## The doctrine, as checks\n1. Agent-readable: one concept per file, ≤ 400 lines (hard 600), a JSDoc file comment; docs come from comments.\n   → npm run check, x docs --check\n2. Agent-operable: every capability through npm scripts, node x or __engine, never only in a page.\n   → command smoke tests, the help() check\n3. Verifiable without a display: the sim runs headless; visual checks are numbers (ID pass, metrics, thumbnails);\n   the HTML/CSS UI is checked through DOM queries. → npm test, x replay, npm run e2e\n4. Assets: procedural first, then text and data; binary only with the owner's approval (fonts are pre-approved).\n   → x check, x esc\n5. Reproducible: fixed 60 Hz, recorded intents, named seeded RNG. Game code writes the standard Math; the sim swaps\n   in fdlibm ports while it steps, so one golden per replay holds in Node and Chromium.\n   → ESLint sim bans, replays, the drift test\n6. WebGPU only, no WebGL fallback. Gameplay runs only on the CPU: sim-side code never imports gfx, the renderer, the\n   DOM or Web Audio, and no GPU result reaches the sim. → ESLint layer rules, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts\n7. Common ground: game code meets npm, Vite, Vitest, Playwright and a public API in plain terms; a bespoke\n   game-facing tool only where nothing fits, with the reason in its file comment. → verifier, ADR-0019\n8. Quality under the hood: game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts; behind them, the best\n   approach you can execute well. Every public export is documented and every error names its fix.\n   → ESLint public-API rule, x docs --check, ADR-0020\n9. Mastery: only the pins tools/deps.json records, and an upgrade is a work package of its own; write the pinned\n   release's idioms (docs/THREE-DELTA.md). → x deps --check, banned names\n10. Discovery first: target this container (Node 24, Chromium 153, WebGPU on SwiftShader); no production hardening\n    outside Phase H, except ADR-0021's touch controls and the one-file playground (ADR-0023 amendment 5).\n\n## Escalate, don't stall\nOwner approval needed (a binary asset, a new dependency, spending money, a repo setting, any other outward action;\npushes, stage PRs, merges and branch deletions are pre-approved, ADR-0021), a principle can't be met, or going\nagainst the doctrine looks best: node x esc open … → post it → wait up to 15 minutes, doing the preferred\nalternative, never the action itself → answered: act on it. No answer: commit, decide, node x esc decide …,\ncontinue, and report later conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, conflict and call.\nThe owner answers in the game: each escalation awaiting their review gets a decide check, in plain words with its\nchoices, in labs/playground/ui/checkList.ts (gone once answered), and whatever only they can judge (feel, looks,\nsound, devices) a try check there, never a checklist in prose alone; their pasted answers come back to record.\n\n## The loop\n1. node x deps --update: adopt compatible releases first, in a commit of their own.\n2. Read your WP in PLAN.md §9 and only the sections it cites, and the file comments of the modules you touch (not\n   whole files). The sources being ported are read-only (node x src).\n3. Edit. The after-edit hook formats and lints the file: fix what it reports.\n4. npm run check → npm test. Browser specs (npm run e2e -- <spec>) only for what only they check that you touched\n   (rendering, the page, the DOM) or a failure you are finding and fixing, never the whole set while you work.\n5. Read out/**/report.json. Open images only when a metric points at one (use the visual-reviewer subagent).\n6. Show it in the box or a fixture test. Update the ledger row in PLAN.md §14 (in ultracode lanes, report it).\n7. Commit \"<area>: <what> (WP-x.y)\". Report the commit, the report paths and any escalations.\n\n## Rules\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Gameplay time is in 60 Hz sim ticks. Randomness comes from named streams.\n- DOCTRINE.md changes only when the owner asks. Never edit out/ or node_modules/ by hand.\n- Never skip, disable or loosen a test to get green. Baselines change only with a written reason.\n- Merge on the quick gate (ADR-0023): squash-merge a PR once node x ci --local is green on its head. GitHub then\n  runs the full run on main; nobody waits on it. node x ci --status (the SessionStart hook prints it) says how\n  main's last one went: a red main is fixed before other work.\n- At each gate and at the end of a goal: push, open the stage's PR, merge it, then wait for main's full run and\n  report it. Start the next stage from the updated main (PLAN.md §11.1).\n- No deployment: the engine is distributed and tested as playground.html, the playground in one self-contained page\n  (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of 2026-11-02; Vercel is gone). A change that alters it commits it rebuilt\n  (node x html --write); the quick gate fails while it is stale (x html --check). The touch controls have no tests\n  (ADR-0021); fix what the owner reports from the file.\n- Never downgrade silently: every downgrade emits an advice code.\n- Ask the entry, never the id: shared code reads registry hooks and never compares ids.\n- No game content in the engine; the box's own content stays in labs/box/, the playground's in labs/playground/\n  (PLAN.md §5.7).\n- Docs are generated from comments: never restate a fact in prose (x docs --check).\n- Deviations from PLAN.md become ADR amendments in docs/decisions/, with the plan edited in the same commit (§11.4).\n- Blocked by the core? Write the smallest core change as a proposal in your WP notes. Don't widen your WP.\n- Say plainly what you did not verify.\n\n## Done means\nVerify commands green; npm run check and npm test green; the specs for what only T2 checks that you touched green;\nshown in the box or a fixture; file comments and docs current; a template for any kind written by hand a third time;\nQA for kinds with a QA family; a verifier review for L-size WPs; no new advice codes; escalations recorded; ledger\nupdated.\n\n## Where things are\nTask guides (docs/INDEX.md opens with them), each read when the task needs it:\n- docs/PROGRESS.md: read first, every session: where things stand, what is next, the open issues.\n- docs/TESTING.md: the tiers, and which unit tests and specs a change selects.\n- docs/PLAYGROUND.md: the playground (Phase 13): the site, the move set and its reference values, the five cameras,\n  the three looks, and how feel and looks are checked.\n- docs/HANDOFF.md: handing the engine to game agents (the prompt, many games at once), starting in a new environment,\n  and the box against my-3d2dge's stress-world.\n- lean/: the default handoff, the engine as a library (ADR-0024 amendment 1); docs/GAME-GUIDE.md and labs/game/, the\n  full workspace's guide and starter. Keep lean/README.md, lean/game.html, the guide and the starter true when a\n  public API changes (`x docs --write` rebuilds lean/my3dge.js and writes the generated blocks).\n  tools/game/new-game.mjs makes a game; `node x engine --test` proves one passes its own gate.\n- labs/box/README.md: the box's scenes, settings and quick loop; adding a scene or a variant (a variant is a new\n  scene file or a settings preset, never a change of meaning in a passing scene).\n- tests/replays/README.md: the replay format; checking, recording and making replays.\n- tools/templates/README.md: the kinds `node x new <kind> <id>` writes from a template and checks (a module, scene,\n  prop, weapon, level…), where each goes, what shows it, and how to add a kind (`node x new` lists them).\n- .claude/skills/: the skills, each loaded when its description says (x-loop after any change, add-entry for a new\n  entry of a kind, stress-box for the box, determinism-debugging when hashes part, animation-authoring, visual-qa,\n  perf-budgets, rapier, three-webgpu, escalation).\n","numLines":115,"startLine":1,"totalLines":115}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0186UDE1uLSSETeeZPkE8X3B","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9KYM6XhGtUrijNyFrv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AirRjtbngzWvwUUGVha1fR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/DOCTRINE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6752,"cache_read_input_tokens":25496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6752},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"9cd2f95b-3b8b-4031-9121-4369973dd574","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:59.236Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9KY5UNQDG1uGXNoffi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AirRjtbngzWvwUUGVha1fR":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/DOCTRINE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AirRjtbngzWvwUUGVha1fR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n2\t\n3\t## How to read this\n4\t\n5\tRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n6\t\n7\t## North Star\n8\t\n9\tBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directly. We deliberately leave behind human-centric development tooling and any development method agents cannot use well. The result: games built faster, more consistently, and at higher quality than with general-purpose engines retrofitted for agents.\n10\t\n11\tAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction remotely; agents fly the plane.\n12\t\n13\tOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n14\t\n15\t## Principles\n16\t\n17\t1. Agent-readable\n18\t   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n19\t\n20\t2. Agent-operable\n21\t   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n22\t\n23\t3. Verifiable without a display\n24\t   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n25\t\n26\t4. Agent-accessible assets\n27\t   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n28\t   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n29\t   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n30\t   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).\n31\t\n32\t   Rationale: agents work best with code and data, and are improving at that faster than legacy asset tools are improving for agents.\n33\t\n34\t5. Reproducible on the development platform\n35\t   On the development platform, identical inputs and seeds produce identical gameplay state, and any gameplay state can be captured, restored, and replayed.\n36\t\n37\t6. WebGPU only\n38\t   WebGPU is the only renderer, in a browser or through a native implementation (ie Vulkan). Gameplay runs only on the CPU. Game UI in HTML/CSS is allowed.\n39\t\n40\t7. Common ground\n41\t   Everything agents work with when building games uses widely used languages, libraries, systems, and patterns. Favor flexibility, ease of use, and established approaches over cutting-edge performance and capabilities.\n42\t\n43\t   Analogy: build the Sherman, not the Tiger.\n44\t\n45\t   Rationale: lean into what agents already do well rather than trying to change it.\n46\t\n47\t8. Quality under the hood\n48\t   Engine internals, meaning code whose API does not appear in game code, use the highest-quality approach their builder can execute well, however complex. Reuse what others have already built well, and invest heavily in what is ours. That complexity stays behind the API: agents need to use the engine well, not read or rebuild it, even when something goes wrong.\n49\t\n50\t   Rationale: the engine is built once and used many times, so better internals raise the quality of everything built on it.\n51\t\n52\t9. Mastery over novelty\n53\t   Use the newest version that is at least 12 months old, so agents know it deeply. A newer version that is backward compatible with a qualifying version, or otherwise works the same way, is adopted immediately, since agents' existing knowledge still applies. Within engine internals covered by principle 8, the builder may use newer versions when they raise quality and it can use them well. Upgrade as newer versions qualify.\n54\t\n55\t10. Discovery first, hardening later\n56\t    Develop and test against a single target platform: the one the development environment runs on. Cross-platform compatibility and hardening happen when a game goes to production.\n57\t\n58\t    Rationale: most of the work is finding something worth shipping.\n59\t\n60\t## Escalation\n61\t\n62\tWhen an action requires human approval or a principle cannot be satisfied, escalate and wait. If there is no response within 15 minutes, commit current work, make the call, and continue. For the rest of that run, or until a human responds, report further conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, every later conflict, and every call made without a response, so each can be found and reviewed.\n63\t\n64\tRationale: progress never stalls indefinitely. The worst case is a review, a change, or a rollback.\n65\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"3407c318-2223-4574-9a39-5ccee028dcbc","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:23:59.275Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/DOCTRINE.md","content":"# Agent-First Game Engine: Doctrine\n\n## How to read this\n\nRead this as a trusted, capable manager building an engine that you and others will use. We don't need to tell you to lock the door at night, keep company secrets off public message boards, avoid burning the place down, or get approval before spending money. Use your judgment. When you think going against this doctrine is best, escalate and explain your reasoning. Rules, principles need to be flexible and we have a process to allow for that.\n\n## North Star\n\nBuild a game engine designed from the ground up for AI coding agents (\"agents\"). Every layer is built so agents can develop, test, inspect, and debug it directly. We deliberately leave behind human-centric development tooling and any development method agents cannot use well. The result: games built faster, more consistently, and at higher quality than with general-purpose engines retrofitted for agents.\n\nAnalogy: build a fully autonomous fighter jet with no cockpit. Removing the pilot removes the constraints a human imposes on the design. Humans give direction remotely; agents fly the plane.\n\nOptimize for the best expected overall output, not flawless software. Solve problems when they actually surface, not in anticipation.\n\n## Principles\n\n1. Agent-readable\n   Code, data, and project structure are optimized for agent comprehension over conventions that serve only humans.\n\n2. Agent-operable\n   Every capability is exposed through machine interfaces. Nothing requires a GUI unless no other option.\n\n3. Verifiable without a display\n   Everything can be run, tested, inspected, and verified without a screen or a human watching.\n\n4. Agent-accessible assets\n   Source assets are created in this order of preference:\n   1. Procedural: code and data that generate the asset.\n   2. Text/data: formats agents read and edit directly.\n   3. Binary: only with human approval. Approved: fonts (WOFF2, TTF, OTF).\n\n   Rationale: agents work best with code and data, and are improving at that faster than legacy asset tools are improving for agents.\n\n5. Reproducible on the development platform\n   On the development platform, identical inputs and seeds produce identical gameplay state, and any gameplay state can be captured, restored, and replayed.\n\n6. WebGPU only\n   WebGPU is the only renderer, in a browser or through a native implementation (ie Vulkan). Gameplay runs only on the CPU. Game UI in HTML/CSS is allowed.\n\n7. Common ground\n   Everything agents work with when building games uses widely used languages, libraries, systems, and patterns. Favor flexibility, ease of use, and established approaches over cutting-edge performance and capabilities.\n\n   Analogy: build the Sherman, not the Tiger.\n\n   Rationale: lean into what agents already do well rather than trying to change it.\n\n8. Quality under the hood\n   Engine internals, meaning code whose API does not appear in game code, use the highest-quality approach their builder can execute well, however complex. Reuse what others have already built well, and invest heavily in what is ours. That complexity stays behind the API: agents need to use the engine well, not read or rebuild it, even when something goes wrong.\n\n   Rationale: the engine is built once and used many times, so better internals raise the quality of everything built on it.\n\n9. Mastery over novelty\n   Use the newest version that is at least 12 months old, so agents know it deeply. A newer version that is backward compatible with a qualifying version, or otherwise works the same way, is adopted immediately, since agents' existing knowledge still applies. Within engine internals covered by principle 8, the builder may use newer versions when they raise quality and it can use them well. Upgrade as newer versions qualify.\n\n10. Discovery first, hardening later\n    Develop and test against a single target platform: the one the development environment runs on. Cross-platform compatibility and hardening happen when a game goes to production.\n\n    Rationale: most of the work is finding something worth shipping.\n\n## Escalation\n\nWhen an action requires human approval or a principle cannot be satisfied, escalate and wait. If there is no response within 15 minutes, commit current work, make the call, and continue. For the rest of that run, or until a human responds, report further conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, every later conflict, and every call made without a response, so each can be found and reviewed.\n\nRationale: progress never stalls indefinitely. The worst case is a review, a change, or a rollback.\n","numLines":65,"startLine":1,"totalLines":65}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AirRjtbngzWvwUUGVha1fR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
