the Wii Remote. Its effect depends on the character. Dr. Stiles' Healing Touch allows him to slow down time, and the cleaner and larger the star, the longer the Healing Touch lasts, and a Touch can only be used once a stage.\n- **Design lineage:** The game is a remake of the game Trauma Center: Under the Knife for the Nintendo DS, although its graphics and mechanics were completely redone to take advantage of the Wii's motion-sensing controls. Additions include a new chapter, tools, and Dr. Nozomi Weaver.\n- **Credits:** Wikipedia lists Daisuke Kanada as director, Katsura Hashino as producer, Masayoshi Suto as designer, and Takaaki Ikeda as programmer (Director(s) | Daisuke Kanada | Producer(s) | Katsura Hashino | Designer(s) | Masayoshi Suto | Programmer(s) | Takaaki Ikeda).\n\n**Gaps:** None of the results describe GUILT in detail. The only mention is a Wikipedia stub heading that points to the Under the Knife article for it. I also found no dedicated design review; the material is mostly consumer reviews and wiki pages. A follow-up search on the GUILT system, or on Under the Knife's GUILT section, would be the next step."],"durationSeconds":6.9423183650000135,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019rRMCjNH3N4B3u9UdQZEU1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
