{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9LTC4ng4VWfZgfcXCZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XeEXuuur9LATFxRMiw4For","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/docs/GAME-GUIDE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15038,"cache_read_input_tokens":32248,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15038},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"4be62706-6613-4425-bd2c-cb1a20699217","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:11.308Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9LSsDHyZGh7akZTX7m","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XeEXuuur9LATFxRMiw4For":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/docs/GAME-GUIDE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XeEXuuur9LATFxRMiw4For","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Making a game with my-3dge\n2\t\n3\tYou are making a game with the my-3dge engine. Read this once, start to finish; after that, look things up (section\n4\t8). A game's AGENTS.md sends you here after `node tools/game/new-game.mjs` made the game. For a model without the\n5\trepository, this one file is also the engine in brief: its public API by area is section 10.\n6\t\n7\t## 1. What you have\n8\t\n9\tmy-3dge is a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier physics, the\n10\tUI in HTML/CSS. Three promises shape everything you write:\n11\t\n12\t- **Play is reproducible.** The rules (the _sim_) run on the CPU at a fixed 60 steps a second. The same seed and the\n13\t  same intents give the same state, bit for bit, in Node and in Chromium; every state has a 16-digit hash.\n14\t- **Nothing needs a screen.** The sim runs headless in Node (tests, `node x sim`). The page renders on WebGPU in a\n15\t  headless Chromium (`node x shot`) and reports what it drew as numbers: which object covers which pixels (the ID\n16\t  pass), the look's metrics, a text thumbnail. You prove a game works with commands, not by looking.\n17\t- **Code and data, not binary files.** Levels are text maps; materials and textures are generators with parameters;\n18\t  sounds are voices and tracker notes; characters are procedural bodies, with a library of readable motion clips.\n19\t\n20\tA game's repository:\n21\t\n22\t| Path                                                             | What it is                                                            |\n23\t| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n24\t| `labs/game/`                                                     | The game: the page, the rules (`scenes/`), the characters (`cast/`), the bot, the replays |\n25\t| `labs/box/`                                                      | The engine's worked example: a hero, crowds, combat, props, looks, sound (read-only)   |\n26\t| `engine/`                                                        | The engine. Game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts            |\n27\t| `tools/`, `x.js`                                                 | The `node x` commands, the lint rules, the templates (tools/templates/)               |\n28\t| `data/anim/`                                                     | The animation library: readable clips, and the engine's actions                       |\n29\t| `docs/`                                                          | This guide, API.md (every public name) and ERRORS.md (every error code, with its fix) |\n30\t| `engine-lock.json`, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`                         | The engine's pinned commit and file hashes; what you needed from the engine           |\n31\t\n32\tEverything but `labs/game/` and the root's game files (AGENTS.md, README.md, package.json…) is the engine's,\n33\tread-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json.\n34\t\n35\t## 2. The model\n36\t\n37\t**Two halves.** The sim is the game's rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them each\n38\tstep, events. Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts and never touches the\n39\tDOM, the GPU, the clock or `Math.random`. The page (labs/game/main.ts) imports engine/index.ts: `createEngine` starts\n40\tthe sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into intents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame`\n41\tfor the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The page reads the sim and never writes it. Lint enforces the split.\n42\t\n43\t**A scene** is the unit `x sim`, the page and the tests run: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings,\n44\tsetup(w), step(w, intents) })`. `setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. One page can\n45\toffer several scenes (`createEngine({ scenes })`, `?scene=<id>` picks one); a running page cannot switch scenes yet,\n46\tso a level select reloads the page with another `?scene`.\n47\t\n48\t**The world, `w`**, is what systems get:\n49\t\n50\t- **Components and entities.** `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields\n51\t  with defaults and units, so the state can be hashed, captured and checked. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an\n52\t  id; `w.query('gem')` returns the live entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)`, `w.add`, `w.remove` and\n53\t  `w.despawn` do the rest. An entity whose component declares `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box until\n54\t  something draws it properly (an actor, a prop, a crowd body).\n55\t- **Systems** run in phases, in this order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`.\n56\t  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` puts a rule after the physics moved the bodies and the\n57\t  readback copied their places into components.\n58\t- **Events.** `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)` listeners. A page\n59\t  reads them from `frame.world.events.trace()` (sounds, effects); emitters burst on them; tests count them.\n60\t- **Time** is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800), never by\n61\t  adding up `w.dt`: floating-point sums drift. `w.timers` schedule in sim seconds.\n62\t- **Randomness** comes from named streams seeded by the run: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n63\t- **Settings** are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum,\n64\t  unit, when: 'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`. Each is reachable from the URL\n65\t  (`?game.gems=20`), from `node x sim … --set game.gems=20`, `node x set` and `__engine.set`, and replays record them.\n66\t\n67\t**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once per step, as plain data: `move` (a world-space\n68\tdirection `[x, z]`), `b` (buttons held), `p` (pressed this step), `aim`, `look`, `cam`, and the game's own\n69\t`'game:<name>'` keys. A player actor reads the step's intents itself; systems get them as their second argument\n70\t(`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). In a page, devices make intents through an action map\n71\t(`actionMap:thirdPerson` by default: WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps, Shift dashes); a map with `look: true` sends\n72\tthe camera's view as `look`, and its `aimWith` fills `aim` (`centre`: what the screen's middle points at; `pointer`:\n73\tthe pointer on the player's floor), and a game with several camera modes gives `createEngine` a map per camera\n74\t(`actionMap: (code) => …`). A bot or a test writes them directly: `engine.step(1, { move: [1, 0] })`. A replay is the\n75\tintents of a run, recorded.\n76\t\n77\t**Proof.** `engine.hash()` digests the whole sim. A replay file (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json) holds a scene,\n78\tseed, settings, the intents at each step that changed them, and the golden hashes at its checkpoints, per platform\n79\t(`linux-x64`). `capture()` and `restore()` copy a run and continue it exactly. If a hash changes, play changed.\n80\t\n81\t## 3. The starter, file by file\n82\t\n83\tThe starter (labs/game/) is a whole small game: run round an arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out.\n84\tGrow your game from it; keep its tests as they are, and change what they check as the game changes.\n85\t\n86\t| File                         | What it shows                                                                              |\n87\t| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n88\t| labs/game/scenes/play.ts     | The rules: a level as text, settings, components (`gem`, `round`), systems, a random stream, events |\n89\t| labs/game/cast/player.ts     | The player: a character on the controller (run, jump, dash) drawn as the humanoid          |\n90\t| labs/game/bot.ts             | A bot that plays through intents, steering down a flow field to the nearest gem            |\n91\t| labs/game/main.ts            | The page: `createEngine`, the HUD in `onFrame`, Enter to play again, Esc's pause menu, the chase camera     |\n92\t| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock     |\n93\t| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play, recorded, with its golden hashes                                      |\n94\t| labs/game/README.md          | The game's own page: what it is, how it plays, the calls made                              |\n95\t\n96\t## 4. What to reach for\n97\t\n98\tThe public names below are in engine/sim-api.ts (sim-side) or engine/index.ts (pages); section 10 lists them all.\n99\tThe box (labs/box/, its README says which scene shows what) has a working use of almost each one.\n100\t\n101\t| You need                       | Reach for                                                                                 | See it in                          |\n102\t| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n103\t| A level                        | A text map in a scene module; `compileLevel`, glyphs (`#` wall, `.` floor, `@` spawn, `w` low wall, `P` pillar, `^` stairs, `=` gallery, `o` dais, `t` brazier), `defineGlyph` for your own; `addLevelBodies`; the page registers the text (`createEngine({ levels })`); a building of storeys: `node x new level`; buildings on a terrain: `defineSite`, `addSiteBodies` | labs/game/scenes/play.ts, labs/box/levels/hall.txt, labs/playground/levels/site.ts |\n104\t| A player or character          | `createCharacter` (speed, jump, dash, coyote time, buffer), `spawnActor`, `measureFeel`; Apex's movement, `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`, or your own: `defineMovement` (`node x new movement`); `node x feel`, `createFeelPanel` | labs/game/cast/player.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts, labs/playground/cast/player.ts |\n105\t| Physics                        | `physics: true` in the scene, `physicsOf(w)`: bodies, `raycast`, `overlap`, `probe`; `addBox`, `addSensor`, `knock`, movers | labs/box/scenes/hall.ts |\n106\t| Props                          | `defineProp` (meshes, a body, hooks), `spawnProp`, `hitProp`; the engine's `prop:crate`, `prop:barrel`, `prop:brazier`, `prop:pillar`; one a player uses or strikes: `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `addInteraction` (`node x new interactable`) | labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts |\n107\t| Rides, doors and switches      | `addLevelLadders`, `addZipline`, `addLauncher`; `addLevelDoors`, `addLevelWindows`, `addSwitch`, `switchGroup`; `setDaytime`, `lightsAtDusk`; `placeActor` | labs/playground/scenes/site.ts |\n108\t| Enemies and crowds             | Crowd bodies (`addAgent`, `updateCrowd`, `knockback`, `killAgent`), steering (`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`), `NavGrid` and `FlowField`, attack tokens, `canSee` | labs/box/cast/walker.ts, labs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts |\n109\t| Combat                         | Moves as data (`defineMove`, `Attack`, `Combo`), hit queries (`coneHits`, `sweepHits`), `damage` and `HEALTH`, projectiles, telegraphs, `reactTo`, `requestHitStop` | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts |\n110\t| Melee, guns and targets        | `addMelee` (punches, kicks, the door kick), `defineWeapon`, `weaponsFor` and `addWeapons` (hip fire, the sights, a climbing recoil, reloads; `node x new weapon`), `defineThing` and `spawnThing` (targets that fall at a hit and stand again; `node x new thing`) | labs/playground/weapons/, labs/playground/cast/dummies.ts |\n111\t| Ragdolls and joints            | `addJoint` (fixed, ball, hinge, slider, rope, spring, as data), `addRagdolls` and `handOff` (a hard blow or a death gives a person's body to physics; it gets up the way it lies), `standUp` | labs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts, labs/playground/cast/partners.ts |\n112\t| Animation                      | Actors animate themselves (`spawnActor({ anim: { build } })`); builds, bodies, the animator's body states, actions (`defineAction`, played on an actor with `actOn`), clips from the library (`playClip`) | labs/box/scenes/clips.ts, `node x anim find` |\n113\t| How it looks                   | Materials and textures as data (`defineMaterial`, `defineTexture`), looks (`?post.look=look:storybook`, `post.combine`, `post.filters`, `defineLook`; `__engine.look()`), skies, lights | labs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts, `node x describe look` |\n114\t| Effects                        | Emitters as data, bursting on sim events (`defineEmitter`); telegraphs; screen shake      | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts           |\n115\t| UI                             | `engine.ui`: `hud(id, text, { at })`, `label`, `damage`, `crosshair`; screens and menus: `createMenu`, `createEngine({ store, modal })` (rebinds kept, a menu's keys out of play), `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen` (a controls screen); the rest plain HTML/CSS | labs/game/main.ts (its pause), labs/playground/ui/, labs/box/menu.ts |\n116\t| Sound                          | Effects and songs as data (`defineSfx`, `defineSong`), the page's `createAudio`; a scene's `sounds` maps events to sounds | labs/box/sounds/effects.ts, labs/box/sounds/page.ts |\n117\t| Cameras                        | `?cam=iso` (`threequarter`, `topdown`, `overhead`, `brawler`, `side`), `?cam3=chase` (`orbit`, `fly`, `fixed`, `first`, `rail`, `shoulder`, `follow`); `__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`; a map per mode: `createEngine({ actionMap: (code) => … })`; a mode of your own: `defineCamera` (`node x new camera`) | labs/game/main.ts, labs/playground/views.ts |\n118\t| Touch controls                 | `defineTouchLayout`, `createEngine({ touchLayout })` (built, not yet tested on phones)   | labs/box/touch.ts                  |\n119\t| A new entry of a kind          | `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>`: the module and its test from a template (`node x new` lists the kinds, tools/templates/README.md says where each goes; a new scene starts as a copy of play.ts instead) | tools/templates/                    |\n120\t\n121\tSaves, high scores and settings go in the page's store (`createStore`, never read by the sim), as labs/game/best.ts\n122\tkeeps the best round.\n123\t\n124\t## 5. Rules that keep play reproducible\n125\t\n126\t- Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts; pages only engine/index.ts.\n127\t  Never import three.js or Rapier directly: the public API re-exports what a game needs.\n128\t- No clocks, timers, `Math.random`, `crypto`, DOM, storage, Web Audio or Node built-ins in sim-side code, and no\n129\t  dynamic `import()`: lint refuses each, and its message names the fix. No network either: what the sim needs from\n130\t  outside arrives as intents or settings. Write `Math.pow`, not `**`. Use the standard `Math`: the sim swaps in\n131\t  portable ports while it steps, so the results match in Node and Chromium.\n132\t- Time in steps; randomness from named streams; entities in `w.query`'s id order.\n133\t- A sim-side module reads no files: a level's text lives in a string in the scene module.\n134\t- Presentation never writes the sim: cameras, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change freely without changing a hash.\n135\t- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n136\t\n137\t**Assets.** Make them with code and data first: geometry, textures and materials as generators with parameters,\n138\tsounds as voices and tracker notes, levels as text. A binary file (an image, a recorded sound, a model) needs the\n139\towner's approval: ask, and once they approve, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json at the game's root as\n140\t`{ \"path\", \"reason\" }`, the reason their answer, who and when; `npm run check` fails a binary listed nowhere. Fonts\n141\tare approved: anywhere in labs/game/, each with its license file beside it.\n142\t\n143\t## 6. Seeing it without a screen\n144\t\n145\tEvery command prints its verdict first, about 20 lines at most, and writes `out/<cmd>/<target>/report.json`;\n146\t`node x help <cmd>` gives its manual.\n147\t\n148\t| Command                                                     | What it does                                                                     |\n149\t| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n150\t| `npm test`                                                  | The game's tests (Vitest), headless                                              |\n151\t| `npm run check`                                             | Types, lint, format, `node x check` (the asset rules; the engine as pinned)       |\n152\t| `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--steps n] [--seed s] [--set k=v] [--feel]` | Runs a scene in Node: its hash, events, entities; `--feel` measures the player |\n153\t| `node x replay labs/game/replays [--browser sim] [--update] [--bisect]` | Checks replays' goldens; `--update` records them; `--bisect` finds where two runs part |\n154\t| `node x shot labs/game [--cam code] [--set k=v] [--png]`    | Renders the page on WebGPU: coverage, visible objects, a text thumbnail; an image only with `--png` |\n155\t| `node x eye labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--cam code] [--steps n]` | What a camera sees of the bodies, in Node with no GPU: a text picture and its legend (`__engine.eye()` in a page) |\n156\t| `node x film labs/game --steps \"…\" [--compare ref]`         | Films scripted play as a contact sheet; `--compare` names the objects whose pixels changed |\n157\t| `node x gallery <kind> [id] --page labs/game [--look id]`   | Films every prop, material, emitter, look, body or move as a labelled contact sheet; `#gallery/<kind>/<id>` opens one on any page |\n158\t| `node x inspect --page labs/game`, then `node x eval \"<js>\"`, `node x dump`, `node x set <key> <value>` | A live headless page to question: the HUD through DOM queries, `__engine` |\n159\t| `node x perf labs/game [--scene id]`                        | Performance as counters (ms per step and frame, draw calls), never frames per second |\n160\t| `node x describe [kind [id]]`                               | The registries: every kind's fields and entries (materials, moves, looks, sounds…) |\n161\t| `node x qa geo tex audio`                                   | Content QA by numbers: the engine's and the game's geometry, props, textures and sounds (labs/game/'s geometry, props, textures and sounds folders); a new or worse violation fails, and one the game accepts goes in its own `tests/baselines/qa-<family>-game.json` with the reason |\n162\t| `node x new <kind> <id>`                                    | A new entry from its template, with its test                                     |\n163\t| `node x audio sfx:<id>` · `node x anim find <words>`        | A sound rendered in Node, as numbers · the clip library searched                 |\n164\t| `node x engine`                                             | Which engine the game is on, and whether its files are as shipped                |\n165\t\n166\tIn a page, `window.__engine` is the inspector, and a headless engine in tests (`createHeadless({ scene, seed,\n167\tsettings })`) has the same members but the rendering ones: `__engine.help()` lists them (`step`, `hash`,\n168\t`capture`, `restore`, `replay`, `input.press`, `input.record`, `set`, `state`, `shot`,\n169\t`stats`…).\n170\t\n171\t## 7. Testing a game\n172\t\n173\t- The starter's test shows the four kinds every game keeps: its golden replay, a run that continues exactly after a\n174\t  capture and a restore, the bot winning on 10 seeds, and a rule (the clock). Tests sit beside their module\n175\t  (`play.test.ts`) and run with `npm test`.\n176\t- Keep the bot as the game grows: teach it each new rule. \"The bot wins on 10 seeds\" keeps proving the game can be won,\n177\t  and finds the seeds that softlock.\n178\t- Check the UI through the DOM: the overlay's elements are `[data-ui]` (`node x inspect`, then `node x eval`).\n179\t- When play changes on purpose, record the goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) and\n180\t  say why in the commit. When a hash changes by surprise, `node x replay <file> --browser sim --bisect` narrows it\n181\t  down: to the step, entity and field where two runs part (Node and Chromium, or Node and Node again), or, when every\n182\t  run agrees but they all left the goldens, to the checkpoints between which they did (then the code, data or a\n183\t  dependency changed what the sim does). The usual causes: a `Math` function held in a variable, `**`, an iteration\n184\t  whose order is not fixed, module state between runs, view settings or presentation reaching the sim, `async` code in\n185\t  a step.\n186\t\n187\t## 8. Looking things up\n188\t\n189\t- **docs/API.md**: every public name with the first sentence of its doc comment, the public API first. The comment at\n190\t  the link is the full manual: grep engine/ for the name.\n191\t- **docs/ERRORS.md**: every error and advice code with its fix. Errors print as `[CODE] message`, and the fix says\n192\t  what to do.\n193\t- **The engine's file comments**: each module opens with what it is for, its rules and a runnable `@example`.\n194\t- **`node x describe`** for data, **`node x help`** for commands, **`__engine.help()`** for the inspector.\n195\t- **The engine's repository** (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge) for its plan, its decisions and its tests.\n196\t\n197\t## 9. When the engine is in your way\n198\t\n199\t1. Work round it in the game: a component, a system, page code of your own.\n200\t2. Write it in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md for an engine agent who has never seen your game: what you tried, what happened\n201\t   (the command, the error code), what you expected, what you did instead. The owner collects these from every game.\n202\t3. Never change the engine quietly: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json. When no\n203\t   workaround exists, patch the file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, with why.\n204\t4. The owner decides when a game moves to a newer engine (`node x engine --update`). It replaces nothing of the\n205\t   game's own (it stops and names what is in the way); the engine's package.json entries win, and it names any it\n206\t   drops or sets back. It says which root files it brought to the engine's templates, and which ones you edited\n207\t   whose templates changed: compare those with tools/game/template/ and take what applies. After it: `npm ci`,\n208\t   `npm run check`, `npm test`, and record the goldens again where play changed.\n209\t\n210\t## 10. The public API by area\n211\t\n212\t<!-- api-map:start: generated by `node x docs --write` from the area comments of engine/sim-api.ts, engine/anim/api.ts and engine/index.ts; edit those, not this section -->\n213\tSim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the\n214\tfirst sentence of its doc comment in docs/API.md, and its whole manual where it is declared.\n215\t\n216\t**Scenes**: defined as data with a setup and a step, run by x sim, x replay and createHeadless.\n217\t`defineScene`, `defineDevAction`; types `Scene`, `SceneSpec`, `SceneAction`, `SceneSound`, `DevActionSpec`.\n218\t\n219\t**The world**: entities, components, systems, timers, events.\n220\t`defineComponent`, `PHASES`; types `World`, `SimRng`, `WorldTimers`, `WorldEvents`, `Entity`, `With`, `SpawnSpec`, `ComponentOf`, `Phase`, `SystemFn`.\n221\t\n222\t**Registries and settings**: content as data, one schema for every setting.\n223\t`defineKind`, `def`, `defineSettings`; types `Entry`, `Kind`, `KindSpec`, `Field`, `Schema`, `SimSettings`, `SettingValue`.\n224\t\n225\t**Intents**: what the sim reads from outside each step.\n226\t`held`, `pressed`, `fromCamera`, `INTENT_KEYS`, `createInputBuffer`, `updateInputBuffer`, `released`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`, `repeated`, `BUFFER_STEPS`, `REPEAT_DELAY`, `REPEAT_RATE`; types `Intents`, `IntentValue`, `CustomIntentKey`, `InputBuffer`.\n227\t\n228\t**Math**: three.js's math classes and the engine's helpers (MathUtils covers clamp, lerp, damp, smoothstep).\n229\t`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Matrix4`, `Euler`, `Box3`, `Sphere`, `Ray`, `Plane`, `Color`, `MathUtils`, `approach`, `angDiff`, `lerpAng`, `approachAng`, `smoothDamp`, `ease`, `swingTwist`.\n230\t\n231\t**Randomness**: seeded streams (the world's are w.rng('<stream>')).\n232\t`Rng`.\n233\t\n234\t**Events**: emitted with w.emit, heard with w.events.on.\n235\ttypes `EventMap`, `Listener`, `TraceRecord`.\n236\t\n237\t**Levels**: a legend of glyphs, level text compiled to plain data (heights, solid grid, colliders, meshes, spawns).\n238\t`compileLevel`, `validateLevel`, `defineGlyph`, `defineLevel`, `getLevel`, `floorHeight`, `topHeight`, `solidAt`, `tileAt`, `LEVEL_FORMAT`; types `CompileOptions`, `LevelSource`, `LevelProblem`, `Glyph`, `GlyphSpec`, `LevelEntry`, `CompiledLevel`, `LevelGlyph`, `BoxCollider`, `ColliderDescriptor`, `GroundDescriptor`, `MeshDescriptor`, `SpawnPoint`, `LightSpot`, `TileRect`, `Point3`.\n239\t\n240\t**Animation (WP 3.4)**: the humanoid skeleton (a root plus 23 joints) and its builds, poses with three.js quaternions, blending under bone masks, additive layers, forward kinematics, SVG contact sheets and the pose checks.\n241\t`JOINTS`, `SOCKETS`, `MASKS`, `createSkeleton`, `boneMask`, `defineBuild`, `getBuild`, `Pose`, `blendPoses`, `makeAdditive`, `addPose`, `hashPose`, `forwardKinematics`, `createWorldPose`, `jointPosition`, `jointRotation`, `jointMatrix`, `jointToWorld`, `socketPosition`, `tipPosition`, `animSheet`, `animCheck`, `twoBone`, `twoBoneRot`, `LIMBS`, `aim`, `AIM_CHAIN`, `footPlant`, `createFootPlantState`, `FOOT_LOCK`, `FOOT_PLANT`, `FootPlant`, `queryGround`, `handTo`; types `JointName`, `SocketName`, `MaskName`, `Skeleton`, `BoneMask`, `Build`, `BuildSpec`, `JointRef`, `BlendOptions`, `WorldPose`, `Sheet`, `SheetFrame`, `SheetOptions`, `SheetView`, `AnimCheckSpec`, `AnimCheckReport`, `AnimProblem`, `RigidPair`, `PointName`, `TwoBoneOptions`, `LimbEnd`, `Limb`, `AimOptions`, `AimLink`, `FootPlantOptions`, `FootPlantState`, `FootLock`, `FootPlantLayerOptions`, `GroundQueries`, `GroundProbe`, `GroundHit`.\n242\t\n243\t**The procedural humanoid (WP 3.6)**: my-3d2dge's gait, idle, lean, arm swing, held poses and stances as effector targets written with B(forward, right, up), solved onto the skeleton; a fixed step and a seeded stream.\n244\t`Humanoid`, `POSES`, `STANCES`, `WEAPONS`, `B`, `turnRight`; types `HumanoidOptions`, `HumanoidInput`, `HumanoidState`, `HumanoidWeights`, `BodyMotion`, `PoseName`, `StanceName`, `Weapon`, `Effectors`, `FRU`, `LimbSide`, `Sided`.\n245\t\n246\t**The character animator (WP 3.7)**: a body driven through an explicit layer stack (weights, base, override, solve, then layers in clips, additive, post, physics and secondary), told its body states each step (unknown words warn with the closest), with footsteps, sockets, a trail and detail levels; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n247\t`Animator`, `BODY_STATES`, `checkBodyStates`, `TRAILS`, `SAMPLE_STATES`, `SLOTS`, `LAYER_SLOTS`, `LOD`, `easeWeight`, `HUMANOID_WEIGHTS`; types `AnimatorOptions`, `PoseOptions`, `AnimatorReport`, `AnimEvent`, `TrailSample`, `BodyStateName`, `AnimatorInput`, `TrailName`, `SampleState`, `SlotName`, `LayerSlot`, `AnimLayer`, `WeightRates`, `WeightInfo`.\n248\t\n249\t**Blob and floater rigs (WP 5.3)**: my-3d2dge's squash-and-stretch blob on a minimal skeleton (a volume-preserving squash spring, a smoothed look, wings, its moves as data: bite, leap, pounce, spit) and a floater that hovers over the floor, banks into turns and carries orbiting motes; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n250\t`Blob`, `BLOB`, `BLOB_JOINTS`, `BLOB_LEAP`, `BLOB_MOVES`, `blobMove`, `blobMoveTimes`, `Floater`, `FLOATER`; types `BlobOptions`, `BlobInput`, `BlobState`, `BlobJoint`, `BlobPose`, `BlobLeap`, `BlobMove`, `BlobMoveName`, `BlobMoveFrame`, `FloaterOptions`, `FloaterInput`, `FloaterState`, `FloaterPose`.\n251\t\n252\t**The animation library (WP 8.4)**: readable key-pose clips (format 1 and 2: parse, print, check, the normalized lens, mirroring, the decode to 36 body points), loaded per set; baked to 30 fps rotation tracks on the skeleton, retargeted onto any build (rotation copy aligned at rest, the hip-height ratio, contact IK) or given to three.js as AnimationClip JSON; played by the clip layer in the animator's clip slot, loops at the ground speed, with shardfall's walk-run switch and strike time.\n253\t`parseClip`, `parseClipSet`, `validateClip`, `poseError`, `poseMeanError`, `clipText`, `normalizedLens`, `mirrorClip`, `decodePose`, `samplePose`, `POSE_POINTS`, `POINT_INDEX`, `bakeClip`, `sampleBaked`, `travelAt`, `BAKE_FPS`, `retargetPose`, `clipJSON`, `hipRatio`, `standingHipHeight`, `restSkeleton`, `restPoints`, `restBuild`, `RETARGET`, `loadClipLibrary`, `clipPace`, `walkRate`, `runOverWalk`, `strikeTime`, `GROUND_SPEED`, `STRIKE`, `ClipLayer`, `CLIP_LAYER`; types `ClipSet`, `ClipSource`, `RestBody`, `ClipWarning`, `Clip`, `ClipKey`, `KeyPose`, `LimbPose`, `ClipEvent`, `ClipContact`, `BakedClip`, `RetargetOptions`, `AnimationClipJSON`, `ClipLibrary`, `ClipOrigin`, `ClipLayerOptions`, `ClipPlay`, `ClipSlot`, `ClipLayerState`, `ClipLayerEvent`, `ClipStepInput`.\n254\t\n255\t**Moves (WP 6.1)**: moves as data in metres (the kind move: my-3d2dge's 24, converted by Appendix C's table), timelines whose events carry leftover time, attacks and combos with hit windows, sweeps and root motion, moves played on a humanoid through its body state (the arm path), moves at a moment for previews, measured hit shapes, the cone helpers and a hit's knockback velocity.\n256\t`defineMove`, `getMove`, `resolveMove`, `MOVE_PHASES`, `LEGACY_FIELDS`, `fromLegacy`, `Timeline`, `Attack`, `Combo`, `moveAt`, `measureMove`, `strikePart`, `strikeSegment`, `inArc`, `inHitShape`, `knockbackVelocity`; types `Move`, `MoveSpec`, `MoveInput`, `MovePhase`, `LegacyMove`, `TimelinePhase`, `TimelineMark`, `TimelineEvent`, `TimelineState`, `AttackState`, `ComboState`, `Sweep`, `HitShape`, `StrikeBody`, `StrikeAnimator`, `ArmState`.\n257\t\n258\t**Reactions and secondary motion (WP 6.2)**: a directional flinch, knockdown, death and the get-up chosen by how the body lies (face up or face down), stepped in the sim; verlet chains (hair, tails) with bone-capsule colliders, a floor probe and a rest shape, a cape of two chains, and the squash spring as a volume-preserving root scale, drawn by an animator layer in the view.\n259\t`Reactions`, `REACTION`, `REACTIONS`, `lyingOf`, `Chain`, `CHAIN`, `Cape`, `Secondary`, `HAIR`, `CAPE`, `TAIL`, `SQUASH`, `squashScale`, `BODY_CAPSULES`, `bodyCapsules`; types `ReactionOptions`, `ReactionState`, `ReactionBody`, `ReactionName`, `Lying`, `ChainOptions`, `ChainTuning`, `BoneCapsule`, `CapeOptions`, `SecondaryOptions`.\n260\t\n261\t**Animation checks (WP 6.6)**: the state matrix (each body state held at each facing, from rest and back to rest) and animCheckBody, which checks a whole body over it: every pose, the same hashes twice, 1/60 s against 1/120 s; and the camera presets sheets of it are drawn from.\n262\t`stateMatrix`, `STATE_MATRIX`, `animCheckBody`, `SHEET_CAMERAS`; types `MatrixState`, `MatrixRun`, `StateMatrixOptions`, `BodyCheckOptions`, `BodyCheckReport`, `AnimCheckName`.\n263\t\n264\t**Actions (WP 8.7)**: multi-beat sequences a body plays, beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and squashes with held contact frames, events, flags and cancel windows; the engine's set is data/anim/actions/, which loadActions defines.\n265\t`Actions`, `defineAction`, `getAction`, `loadActions`, `actionCheck`, `ACTION`, `FLIP_AXES`, `EFFECTORS`, `CHANNELS`; types `ActionsOptions`, `ActionsState`, `Action`, `Beat`, `ActionSpec`.\n266\t\n267\t**Clip sets in the registry (WP 8.9)**: the kind clipset, one entry per set of readable clips, which a page or tool registers from data/anim/sets/ (defineClipSet), and the clip layer of an entity kept as plain data, loaded from it at every use (ClipLayerPool), as an actor's component clips holds it.\n268\t`defineClipSet`, `clipSetFromFiles`, `getClipSet`, `clipSetId`, `clipSetIds`, `defineSetBuild`, `CLIPSET_FIELDS`, `CLIPS_FIELDS`, `emptyClipState`, `ClipLayerPool`; types `ClipSetEntry`, `ClipsData`.\n269\t\n270\t**The quadruped rig (WP 13.21)**: a dog on the planted gait for a crowd body (the rig `quadruped`), its state plain data, its pose the joints its body is drawn on, and its rig contract for the anim QA.\n271\t`Quadruped`, `QUADRUPED`, `QUADRUPED_LEGS`, `QUADRUPED_RIG`; types `QuadrupedOptions`, `QuadrupedInput`, `QuadrupedState`, `QuadrupedPose`, `QuadrupedBone`.\n272\t\n273\t**Physics (WP 3.1)**: Rapier behind plain terms. A scene with `physics: true` has a physics world: bodies by id, raycasts, shape casts, overlaps and ground probes, collision groups by name, a level's bodies; presentation reads it through the read-only QueryView.\n274\t`physicsOf`, `COLLISION_GROUPS`, `PHYSICS_SETTINGS`, `addLevelBodies`, `queryViewOf`; types `Physics`, `BodySpec`, `ColliderSpec`, `BodyState`, `BodyChange`, `BodyType`, `Shape`, `Vec3Like`, `QuatLike`, `Hit`, `Ground`, `QueryOptions`, `RayOptions`, `ShapeCastOptions`, `OverlapOptions`, `ProbeOptions`, `GroupName`, `QueryView`.\n275\t\n276\t**Characters (WP 3.2)**: actors moved by the character controller (Rapier's, moved across then down), with an action game's feel: acceleration and skid, coyote time, a jump buffer, a variable jump, a wall jump, a dash, pushing props; the feel meter measures how one handles. A body's reactions and an actor's secondary motion as components (WP 7.8), and its actions (`actOn`, WP 13.11).\n277\t`createCharacter`, `CHARACTER_FIELDS`, `CHARACTER_STATE_FIELDS`, `spawnActor`, `removeActor`, `measureFeel`, `reactTo`, `stepReactions`, `REACTION_FIELDS`, `SECONDARY_FIELDS`, `actOn`, `ACTIONS_FIELDS`; types `Character`, `CharacterState`, `ActorSpec`, `ActorComponent`, `ActorComponents`, `FeelReport`, `FeelOptions`, `AnimatorComponent`, `SecondaryComponent`, `ReactionComponents`, `ActionComponents`.\n278\t\n279\t**Levels v2 (WP 4.2)**: the hall's glyphs (gallery =, stairs ^, dais o, brazier t), ramps and daises, hull and cylinder colliders, and the surface tag underfoot.\n280\t`surfaceAt`; types `HullCollider`, `CylinderCollider`, `RampDescriptor`, `DaisDescriptor`, `Side`.\n281\t\n282\t**Navigation and spatial queries (WP 4.3)**: a level's walkable grid under a climb limit (the crowd's, or a hero's jump reach from measureFeel), A* paths smoothed by line of sight, cached flow fields with reach and a look-ahead direction, and a spatial hash on typed arrays for neighbours.\n283\t`NavGrid`, `FlowField`, `NAV_GRID_FIELDS`, `NAV_DIRECTIONS`, `LOOK_AHEAD`, `NEAREST_RINGS`, `NAV_CLIMB`, `SpatialHash`, `SPATIAL_HASH_FIELDS`; types `NavGridOptions`, `FlowOptions`, `GroundPoint`, `SpatialHashOptions`.\n284\t\n285\t**Bodies and props (WP 4.1)**: props of one shape in the prototype's material, torque impulses and the knock; movers, kinematic bodies whose pose is a function of time, carrying riders; sensors and triggers; the lost-body rescue.\n286\t`PROP_MATERIAL`, `addProp`, `addBox`, `addBall`, `addCapsule`, `addCylinder`, `applyTorqueImpulse`, `knock`, `MOVER_FIELDS`, `MOVER`, `moverPose`, `driveBody`, `driveMover`, `addSensor`, `sensorHits`, `TRIGGER_FIELDS`, `updateTrigger`, `rescueLost`; types `PropSpec`, `KnockOptions`, `Mover`, `SensorSpec`, `Trigger`, `RescueLevel`.\n287\t\n288\t**Props as data (WP 4.5)**: the registry kind `prop` (meshes by geometry and material id, a body, a light spot, where a level places it, the hit and use hooks), the engine's crate, barrel, brazier fire and pillar, the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table, bench, statue and lantern (WP 10.3), and their placement.\n289\t`defineProp`, `getProp`, `propCentre`, `levelProps`, `propBody`, `propPose`; types `Prop`, `PropDefinition`, `PropPart`, `PropTouch`, `PropKnock`, `PropPlacement`, `PropBody`, `PropPose`.\n290\t\n291\t**Props in a scene (WP 4.6)**: spawnProp stands a prop in a running scene (its body, and an entity with the component sceneProp whose pose is read back after each step, which the view draws as the prop); hitProp calls its hit hook with the means to knock it; removeProp.\n292\t`spawnProp`, `removeProp`, `hitProp`, `SCENE_PROP_FIELDS`; types `ScenePropComponent`, `ScenePropComponents`.\n293\t\n294\t**Crowd bodies (WP 5.1)**: agents as dynamic capsules and balls driven by the velocities their AI wants (approach limits, only on their feet), knockback and launches, the corpse group and its timers, spawn and despawn queues.\n295\t`CROWD_AGENT_FIELDS`, `CROWD_AGENT`, `CROWD_MATERIAL`, `CROWD_LIMITS`, `CROWD_GROUNDED`, `addAgent`, `removeAgent`, `setVelocity`, `knockback`, `launch`, `killAgent`, `updateCrowd`; types `CrowdAgent`, `AgentState`, `VelocityOptions`, `CrowdOptions`, `CrowdChanges`, `CrowdStepper`.\n296\t\n297\t**Steering kit (WP 5.2)**: the velocity a crowd agent wants (seek and arrive, flow-field following, approach rings, a flyer's hover, a jumper's hop), attack tokens that cap the attackers at once, and awareness by distance and sight.\n298\t`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`, `hover`, `airTime`, `hop`, `FOLLOW`, `APPROACH_RING`, `HOVER`, `HOP`, `ATTACK_TOKENS_FIELDS`, `ATTACK_TOKENS`, `takeToken`, `releaseToken`, `holdsToken`, `keepTokens`, `canSee`, `clearSight`, `SIGHT`; types `SteerPoint`, `FollowOptions`, `ApproachRingOptions`, `HoverOptions`, `AttackTokens`, `SightOptions`.\n299\t\n300\t**Crowd bodies (WP 5.7)**: a crowd member's look and rig (a humanoid in a build, a blob, a floater) in the component crowdBody, its rig stepped in the sim by stepCrowdBody; the stage draws them through a crowd with its level of detail.\n301\t`CROWD_BODY_FIELDS`, `CROWD_BODY`, `stepCrowdBody`; types `CrowdBody`, `CrowdRig`, `CrowdBodyInput`.\n302\t\n303\t**Telegraphs (WP 6.4)**: an attack's warning shape on the floor as data (an arc, a ring, a line or a cone, in metres), and the geometry sim code tests it with as it goes off: a body inside it is hit, one just outside it a near miss.\n304\t`TELEGRAPH_SHAPES`, `defineTelegraph`, `getTelegraph`, `telegraphGap`, `inTelegraph`, `nearMiss`, `telegraphFill`, `coneOf`; types `Telegraph`, `TelegraphSpec`, `TelegraphPlace`, `PointLike`.\n305\t\n306\t**Combat helpers (WP 6.3)**: hit queries against hurt capsules found through a spatial hash (cones with height windows, weapon sweeps), damage on health and crowd bodies (knockback as momentum, launches, death into the corpse group, the event damage), hit-stop handed to the frame clock's budget, and projectiles with swept tests.\n307\t`PROJECTILE_FIELDS`, `PROJECTILE`, `stepProjectiles`, `capsuleCast`, `inCone`, `coneHits`, `sweepTouches`, `sweepHits`, `physicsWalls`, `HEALTH_FIELDS`, `HEALTH`, `damage`, `requestHitStop`; types `Projectile`, `ProjectileState`, `ProjectileStep`, `ProjectileHit`, `HurtCapsule`, `HurtSet`, `WallTest`, `WallContact`, `Health`, `DamageTarget`, `HitSpec`, `DamageEvent`, `DamageEvents`.\n308\t\n309\t**Melee (WP 13.19)**: Apex's punch, kicks and the door kick, a lunge to the target in reach, one hit a swing landing at its moment with its damage and hit-stop; the fan of rays that finds what a strike reaches.\n310\t`MELEE`, `MELEE_MOVES`, `MELEE_FIELDS`, `addMelee`, `meleeTargets`, `fanHits`; types `MeleeState`, `MeleeReach`, `Fan`, `FanHit`.\n311\t\n312\t**Firearms (WP 13.20)**: the kind `weapon`, a player's weapons drawn and holstered, hip fire and the sights, hitscan shots with spread and a climbing recoil, reloads.\n313\t`WEAPON_FIELDS`, `defineWeapon`, `getWeapon`, `recoilStep`, `spreadOf`, `WEAPONS_FIELDS`, `weaponsFor`, `addWeapons`; types `WeaponKind`, `WeaponSpec`, `WeaponsState`.\n314\t\n315\t**Ragdolls (WP 10.2)**: joints as data, a humanoid's body handed off to physics on a hard hit or a death, lying still and getting up the way it lies.\n316\t`addJoint`, `removeJoint`, `hasJoint`, `jointBodies`, `RAGDOLL_SIM`, `RAGDOLL_FIELDS`, `addRagdolls`, `handOff`, `removeRagdollOf`, `standUp`; types `JointKind`, `JointSpec`, `RagdollState`, `RagdollHit`.\n317\t\n318\t**A town's daily round (WP 13.21)**: routes walked on a schedule, and stepping aside for someone in the way.\n319\t`ROUTE_FIELDS`, `followRoute`, `ASIDE`, `stepAside`; types `RouteStop`, `RouteState`, `RouteLeg`.\n320\t\n321\t**A crowd body's ground (WP 13.21)**: the physics world's, for a gait's feet (a quadruped's).\n322\t`crowdGround`.\n323\t\n324\t**Clips on actors (WP 8.9)**: the clip layer in an actor's animator as sim data, the component clips, stepped at the actor's ground speed and drawn over its body by the view; its events come as `clip`. spawnActor's `body` names the body it is drawn as (body:mannequin).\n325\t`addClips`, `playClip`, `stopClip`, `seekClip`, `locomoteClips`, `clipLayerOf`; types `ClipsSpec`, `PuppetComponent`.\n326\t\n327\t**Movement profiles (WP 13.3)**: the registry kind `movement`, and the shooter model (Apex's) a profile gives a character.\n328\t`defineMovement`, `getMovement`, `listMovements`, `MODEL_FIELDS`, `MOTION_FIELDS`; types `MovementProfile`, `MovementSpec`, `MovementModel`, `MovementModelSpec`, `MotionState`, `MotionFeel`.\n329\t\n330\t**The feel kit, sim-side (WP 13.4)**: the shooter model's live tuning, `movement.<group>.<field>` (a setting off its default replaces that number in every model character's model, from the next step), and an actor placed at rest (a scene's dev action: a feel course's start).\n331\t`MOVEMENT_SETTINGS`, `movementOverrides`, `tuneCharacter`, `placeActor`; types `MovementOverride`.\n332\t\n333\t**Rides (WP 13.7)**: ziplines, ladders and launchers as world data (engine/world/traverse.ts), placed in a scene (engine/sim/rides.ts) and ridden by the shooter model (engine/physics/movement/ride.ts, ladder.ts): a zipline and the poles under its anchors, the redeploy balloon's line and its skydive, a ladder (or every ladder a level's ladder glyph `|` stands), a jump pad or a trampoline. Use is the button `interact`; the look's pitch picks a vertical zipline's way and a skydive's dive.\n334\t`addZipline`, `addLadder`, `addLauncher`, `addLevelLadders`, `ridesOf`, `levelLadders`; types `ZiplineSpec`, `LadderSpec`, `LauncherSpec`, `Rides`, `RideCable`, `RideLadder`, `RideLauncher`.\n335\t\n336\t**Storeys (WP 13.13)**: format 2, levels written as storeys, read a storey at a time or in 3D: the highest surface at or under a point, its storey (by height, or by that surface) and its room.\n337\t`LEVEL_FORMAT_2`, `storeyView`, `floorBelow`, `surfaceBelow`, `storeyAt`, `storeyBelow`, `roomAt`; types `StoreyDescriptor`, `RoomDescriptor`, `OpeningDescriptor`, `RoofDescriptor`.\n338\t\n339\t**Terrain (WP 10.5)**: the registry kind `terrain`, a seeded heightfield, its queries, its body and its navigation.\n340\t`defineTerrain`, `getTerrain`, `makeTerrain`, `terrainProblems`, `TERRAIN_FIELDS`, `terrainHeight`, `terrainGradient`, `terrainNormal`, `terrainGrade`, `terrainSurface`, `scatterTerrain`, `addTerrainBody`, `terrainNavGrid`, `terrainNavSurface`; types `Terrain`, `TerrainSpec`, `TerrainEntry`, `TerrainHill`, `TerrainFlat`, `ScatterRule`, `TerrainPlace`, `TerrainNavOptions`, `NavSurface`, `NavSource`.\n341\t\n342\t**Sites (WP 13.15)**: levels standing on a terrain, each placed and turned in quarter turns, the terrain cut under them; the queries over all of it, the bodies, the ladders and the navigation over every storey and the terrain.\n343\t`defineSite`, `getSite`, `makeSite`, `SITE_FIELDS`, `toWorld`, `toLocal`, `siteFloorBelow`, `siteSurfaceBelow`, `siteStructureAt`, `siteSolidAt`, `siteClearSight`, `siteRescueGrid`, `createSiteNav`, `addSiteBodies`, `addSiteLadders`; types `Site`, `SiteSpec`, `SiteStructure`, `StructureSpec`, `SiteRescueGrid`, `SiteNav`, `SiteNavOptions`.\n344\t\n345\t**The scene's props as navigation blocks (WP 13.21)**: what a site's walkers go round.\n346\t`propObstacles`; types `NavBlock`.\n347\t\n348\t**Use (WP 13.16)**: the system `interact` (a player's use of the thing in reach and in view, with its prompt; carrying and throwing a light prop), the kinds `usable` and `strikable`, and the body-to-entity lookup.\n349\t`addInteraction`, `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `strikeEntity`, `USE_REACH`, `CARRY_MASS`, `THROW_SPEED`, `propOfBody`; types `UsableSpec`, `InteractorComponent`, `StrikeForce`, `SpawnPropOptions`, `PropHealthComponent`, `PropLightComponent`.\n350\t\n351\t**Doors (WP 13.16)**: hung on a hinge or sliding, opened away from the user, locked, blocked, burst open and broken.\n352\t`addDoor`, `addLevelDoors`, `useDoor`, `strikeDoor`, `breakDoor`, `DOOR_SWING`; types `DoorSpec`, `DoorComponent`.\n353\t\n354\t**Windows and breakables (WP 13.16)**: props with health and their breaking, a level's window panes, a bolt's strike.\n355\t`addLevelWindows`, `addBreakables`, `damageProp`, `breakProp`, `strikeBody`, `BREAK_SPEED`.\n356\t\n357\t**Switches (WP 13.16)**: a room's lights and the garage door, as sim state the view reads.\n358\t`addSwitch`, `switchGroup`, `flipSwitch`; types `SwitchSpec`, `SwitchComponent`.\n359\t\n360\t**Strikes (WP 13.19)**: a strikable entry by entity (the move a melee plays on it: a door's kick), what takes one, and whose a body is (a prop's or an actor's, WP 13.20).\n361\t`strikeEntry`, `takesStrike`, `entityOfBody`; types `StrikeEntry`.\n362\t\n363\t**Light and time (WP 13.23)**: the world's clock, and the light groups it switches at dusk and dawn.\n364\t`setDaytime`, `daytimeOf`, `lightsAtDusk`, `litAt`, `DUSK`, `DAWN`; types `DaytimeComponent`, `DuskComponent`.\n365\t\n366\t**Things (WP 10.4)**: what can be hit, as a range's targets and a ring's dummies: the kind `thing` (its prop, hit points, rise, track and hooks), a thing's state (hits, down and up, its place on its track), and things in a scene (spawned as their prop, struck as hits, stood back up and driven along their tracks by the system `things`).\n367\t`defineThing`, `getThing`, `thingState`, `hitThing`, `stepThing`, `thingAt`, `THING_FIELDS`, `THING_KIND_FIELDS`, `spawnThing`, `strikeThing`; types `Thing`, `ThingKind`, `ThingSpec`, `ThingTouch`, `ThingHit`, `ThingOutcome`, `ThingStanding`.\n368\t\n369\t**Projectile patterns (WP 10.4)**: a shot emitter's volleys (aimed, forward, ring, spiral, random) and timing (delay, interval, bursts and their pause, range), and the shots it looses over WP 6.3's projectiles.\n370\t`SHOT_EMITTER`, `SHOT_EMITTER_FIELDS`, `PATTERNS`, `MOST_VOLLEYS`, `volley`, `stepVolleys`, `loose`, `direction`, `headingTo`; types `ShotEmitter`, `Pattern`, `VolleyAim`, `LooseOptions`.\n371\t\n372\t**Awareness (WP 10.4)**: beyond WP 5.2's sight, the noises an agent hears and its memory of where it last saw or heard.\n373\t`AWARENESS`, `AWARENESS_FIELDS`, `AWARENESS_STATES`, `sense`, `hears`, `earshot`; types `Awareness`, `AwarenessState`, `Noise`, `SenseInput`, `Sensed`.\n374\t\n375\t**Springs and blends (WP 10.1)**: numbers, vectors and turns on exact damped springs with kicks; named weights eased up and down; the pose stack, angles averaged round the circle, a pitch and a yaw as a direction.\n376\t`Spring1`, `Spring3`, `SpringQuat`, `StateWeights`, `layerPose`, `blendAngles`, `aimDirection`; types `SpringOptions`, `BlendRates`, `PoseLayer`.\n377\t\n378\t**Legs and bodies (WP 10.1)**: the digitigrade leg's reverse knee; sway chains and lagging parts; the planted gait for N legs in groups, its feet on raycast ground or gripping walls; path chains following their head.\n379\t`digitigrade`, `SwayChain`, `LagPart`, `LegGait`, `PathChain`; types `DigitigradeLeg`, `DigitigradeFrame`, `DigitigradeSolve`, `SwayOptions`, `LagOptions`, `GaitLeg`, `GaitOptions`, `GaitFoot`, `GaitProbe`, `GaitBody`, `PathOptions`.\n380\t\n381\t**Faces and floating parts (WP 10.1)**: looking within bounds and blinking from a named stream; hovering, banking, leaning and folding; orbiters and detached parts; pose history played back late; extras on sockets; tip trails.\n382\t`LookAt`, `Blink`, `Hover`, `Orbiters`, `DetachedPart`, `PoseHistory`, `socketMatrix`, `placeExtras`, `along`, `alongDirection`, `TipTrail`; types `LookAtOptions`, `BlinkOptions`, `HoveringOptions`, `OrbitOptions`, `DetachedOptions`, `HistoryOptions`, `Socket`, `SocketJoints`, `TrailOptions`, `TipSample`.\n383\t\n384\t**Reach and the rig contract (WP 10.1)**: an attack's reach measured off its animation; what a procedural rig names (its points, bones, feet and states) and the check it passes, as anim.check holds the humanoid.\n385\t`measureReach`, `driveRig`, `rigCheck`, `rigClosed`; types `ReachPose`, `ReachOptions`, `Reach`, `ProcRig`, `ProcRigBody`, `RigRun`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"d7020622-16c3-44cf-866d-9a5be7622ff6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:11.557Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/engine/docs/GAME-GUIDE.md","content":"# Making a game with my-3dge\n\nYou are making a game with the my-3dge engine. Read this once, start to finish; after that, look things up (section\n8). A game's AGENTS.md sends you here after `node tools/game/new-game.mjs` made the game. For a model without the\nrepository, this one file is also the engine in brief: its public API by area is section 10.\n\n## 1. What you have\n\nmy-3dge is a 3D web game engine built for AI coding agents: TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier physics, the\nUI in HTML/CSS. Three promises shape everything you write:\n\n- **Play is reproducible.** The rules (the _sim_) run on the CPU at a fixed 60 steps a second. The same seed and the\n  same intents give the same state, bit for bit, in Node and in Chromium; every state has a 16-digit hash.\n- **Nothing needs a screen.** The sim runs headless in Node (tests, `node x sim`). The page renders on WebGPU in a\n  headless Chromium (`node x shot`) and reports what it drew as numbers: which object covers which pixels (the ID\n  pass), the look's metrics, a text thumbnail. You prove a game works with commands, not by looking.\n- **Code and data, not binary files.** Levels are text maps; materials and textures are generators with parameters;\n  sounds are voices and tracker notes; characters are procedural bodies, with a library of readable motion clips.\n\nA game's repository:\n\n| Path                                                             | What it is                                                            |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `labs/game/`                                                     | The game: the page, the rules (`scenes/`), the characters (`cast/`), the bot, the replays |\n| `labs/box/`                                                      | The engine's worked example: a hero, crowds, combat, props, looks, sound (read-only)   |\n| `engine/`                                                        | The engine. Game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts            |\n| `tools/`, `x.js`                                                 | The `node x` commands, the lint rules, the templates (tools/templates/)               |\n| `data/anim/`                                                     | The animation library: readable clips, and the engine's actions                       |\n| `docs/`                                                          | This guide, API.md (every public name) and ERRORS.md (every error code, with its fix) |\n| `engine-lock.json`, `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`                         | The engine's pinned commit and file hashes; what you needed from the engine           |\n\nEverything but `labs/game/` and the root's game files (AGENTS.md, README.md, package.json…) is the engine's,\nread-only: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json.\n\n## 2. The model\n\n**Two halves.** The sim is the game's rules: entities holding components (plain data), systems that change them each\nstep, events. Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts and never touches the\nDOM, the GPU, the clock or `Math.random`. The page (labs/game/main.ts) imports engine/index.ts: `createEngine` starts\nthe sim, turns the keyboard, mouse, pads and touch into intents, draws every frame on WebGPU, and calls your `onFrame`\nfor the game's own presentation (the HUD, sound). The page reads the sim and never writes it. Lint enforces the split.\n\n**A scene** is the unit `x sim`, the page and the tests run: `defineScene('game:play', { level, physics, settings,\nsetup(w), step(w, intents) })`. `setup` spawns entities and adds systems; `step` runs first in every step. One page can\noffer several scenes (`createEngine({ scenes })`, `?scene=<id>` picks one); a running page cannot switch scenes yet,\nso a level select reloads the page with another `?scene`.\n\n**The world, `w`**, is what systems get:\n\n- **Components and entities.** `defineComponent('gem', { description, fields })` declares a component: typed fields\n  with defaults and units, so the state can be hashed, captured and checked. `w.spawn({ gem: { x, z } })` returns an\n  id; `w.query('gem')` returns the live entities holding it, in id order; `w.get(id)`, `w.add`, `w.remove` and\n  `w.despawn` do the rest. An entity whose component declares `x` and `z` in metres is drawn as a stand-in box until\n  something draws it properly (an actor, a prop, a crowd body).\n- **Systems** run in phases, in this order: `intents`, `ai`, `anim`, `physics`, `readback`, `rules`.\n  `w.systems.add('game.collect', collect, { phase: 'rules' })` puts a rule after the physics moved the bodies and the\n  readback copied their places into components.\n- **Events.** `w.emit('gem', payload)` delivers at the end of the step to `w.events.on('gem', …)` listeners. A page\n  reads them from `frame.world.events.trace()` (sounds, effects); emitters burst on them; tests count them.\n- **Time** is steps: `w.tick`, `w.dt` (1/60 s), `w.hz`. Count a duration in whole steps (30 s is 1,800), never by\n  adding up `w.dt`: floating-point sums drift. `w.timers` schedule in sim seconds.\n- **Randomness** comes from named streams seeded by the run: `w.rng('gems').int(0, 9)`, `.range(a, b)`, `.chance(p)`.\n- **Settings** are the game's tunable numbers: `defineSettings({ 'game.gems': { type, default, minimum, maximum,\n  unit, when: 'scene', description } })`, read with `w.settings.get('game.gems')`. Each is reachable from the URL\n  (`?game.gems=20`), from `node x sim … --set game.gems=20`, `node x set` and `__engine.set`, and replays record them.\n\n**Intents** are everything the sim reads from outside, once per step, as plain data: `move` (a world-space\ndirection `[x, z]`), `b` (buttons held), `p` (pressed this step), `aim`, `look`, `cam`, and the game's own\n`'game:<name>'` keys. A player actor reads the step's intents itself; systems get them as their second argument\n(`held(intents, 'jump')`, `pressed(intents, 'attack')`). In a page, devices make intents through an action map\n(`actionMap:thirdPerson` by default: WASD or the arrows run, Space jumps, Shift dashes); a map with `look: true` sends\nthe camera's view as `look`, and its `aimWith` fills `aim` (`centre`: what the screen's middle points at; `pointer`:\nthe pointer on the player's floor), and a game with several camera modes gives `createEngine` a map per camera\n(`actionMap: (code) => …`). A bot or a test writes them directly: `engine.step(1, { move: [1, 0] })`. A replay is the\nintents of a run, recorded.\n\n**Proof.** `engine.hash()` digests the whole sim. A replay file (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json) holds a scene,\nseed, settings, the intents at each step that changed them, and the golden hashes at its checkpoints, per platform\n(`linux-x64`). `capture()` and `restore()` copy a run and continue it exactly. If a hash changes, play changed.\n\n## 3. The starter, file by file\n\nThe starter (labs/game/) is a whole small game: run round an arena collecting every gem before the clock runs out.\nGrow your game from it; keep its tests as they are, and change what they check as the game changes.\n\n| File                         | What it shows                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.ts     | The rules: a level as text, settings, components (`gem`, `round`), systems, a random stream, events |\n| labs/game/cast/player.ts     | The player: a character on the controller (run, jump, dash) drawn as the humanoid          |\n| labs/game/bot.ts             | A bot that plays through intents, steering down a flow field to the nearest gem            |\n| labs/game/main.ts            | The page: `createEngine`, the HUD in `onFrame`, Enter to play again, Esc's pause menu, the chase camera     |\n| labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts | The tests: the golden replay, capture and restore, the bot wins on 10 seeds, the clock     |\n| labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json | The bot's play, recorded, with its golden hashes                                      |\n| labs/game/README.md          | The game's own page: what it is, how it plays, the calls made                              |\n\n## 4. What to reach for\n\nThe public names below are in engine/sim-api.ts (sim-side) or engine/index.ts (pages); section 10 lists them all.\nThe box (labs/box/, its README says which scene shows what) has a working use of almost each one.\n\n| You need                       | Reach for                                                                                 | See it in                          |\n| ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| A level                        | A text map in a scene module; `compileLevel`, glyphs (`#` wall, `.` floor, `@` spawn, `w` low wall, `P` pillar, `^` stairs, `=` gallery, `o` dais, `t` brazier), `defineGlyph` for your own; `addLevelBodies`; the page registers the text (`createEngine({ levels })`); a building of storeys: `node x new level`; buildings on a terrain: `defineSite`, `addSiteBodies` | labs/game/scenes/play.ts, labs/box/levels/hall.txt, labs/playground/levels/site.ts |\n| A player or character          | `createCharacter` (speed, jump, dash, coyote time, buffer), `spawnActor`, `measureFeel`; Apex's movement, `{ movement: 'movement:apex' }`, or your own: `defineMovement` (`node x new movement`); `node x feel`, `createFeelPanel` | labs/game/cast/player.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts, labs/playground/cast/player.ts |\n| Physics                        | `physics: true` in the scene, `physicsOf(w)`: bodies, `raycast`, `overlap`, `probe`; `addBox`, `addSensor`, `knock`, movers | labs/box/scenes/hall.ts |\n| Props                          | `defineProp` (meshes, a body, hooks), `spawnProp`, `hitProp`; the engine's `prop:crate`, `prop:barrel`, `prop:brazier`, `prop:pillar`; one a player uses or strikes: `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `addInteraction` (`node x new interactable`) | labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts |\n| Rides, doors and switches      | `addLevelLadders`, `addZipline`, `addLauncher`; `addLevelDoors`, `addLevelWindows`, `addSwitch`, `switchGroup`; `setDaytime`, `lightsAtDusk`; `placeActor` | labs/playground/scenes/site.ts |\n| Enemies and crowds             | Crowd bodies (`addAgent`, `updateCrowd`, `knockback`, `killAgent`), steering (`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`), `NavGrid` and `FlowField`, attack tokens, `canSee` | labs/box/cast/walker.ts, labs/box/scenes/crowd-1000.ts |\n| Combat                         | Moves as data (`defineMove`, `Attack`, `Combo`), hit queries (`coneHits`, `sweepHits`), `damage` and `HEALTH`, projectiles, telegraphs, `reactTo`, `requestHitStop` | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts, labs/box/cast/hero.ts |\n| Melee, guns and targets        | `addMelee` (punches, kicks, the door kick), `defineWeapon`, `weaponsFor` and `addWeapons` (hip fire, the sights, a climbing recoil, reloads; `node x new weapon`), `defineThing` and `spawnThing` (targets that fall at a hit and stand again; `node x new thing`) | labs/playground/weapons/, labs/playground/cast/dummies.ts |\n| Ragdolls and joints            | `addJoint` (fixed, ball, hinge, slider, rope, spring, as data), `addRagdolls` and `handOff` (a hard blow or a death gives a person's body to physics; it gets up the way it lies), `standUp` | labs/box/scenes/ragdoll.ts, labs/playground/cast/partners.ts |\n| Animation                      | Actors animate themselves (`spawnActor({ anim: { build } })`); builds, bodies, the animator's body states, actions (`defineAction`, played on an actor with `actOn`), clips from the library (`playClip`) | labs/box/scenes/clips.ts, `node x anim find` |\n| How it looks                   | Materials and textures as data (`defineMaterial`, `defineTexture`), looks (`?post.look=look:storybook`, `post.combine`, `post.filters`, `defineLook`; `__engine.look()`), skies, lights | labs/box/looks/torch-lit.ts, `node x describe look` |\n| Effects                        | Emitters as data, bursting on sim events (`defineEmitter`); telegraphs; screen shake      | labs/box/scenes/fight.ts           |\n| UI                             | `engine.ui`: `hud(id, text, { at })`, `label`, `damage`, `crosshair`; screens and menus: `createMenu`, `createEngine({ store, modal })` (rebinds kept, a menu's keys out of play), `input.bindingsFor`, `rebind`, `listen` (a controls screen); the rest plain HTML/CSS | labs/game/main.ts (its pause), labs/playground/ui/, labs/box/menu.ts |\n| Sound                          | Effects and songs as data (`defineSfx`, `defineSong`), the page's `createAudio`; a scene's `sounds` maps events to sounds | labs/box/sounds/effects.ts, labs/box/sounds/page.ts |\n| Cameras                        | `?cam=iso` (`threequarter`, `topdown`, `overhead`, `brawler`, `side`), `?cam3=chase` (`orbit`, `fly`, `fixed`, `first`, `rail`, `shoulder`, `follow`); `__engine.camera.set(code, { blend: true })`; a map per mode: `createEngine({ actionMap: (code) => … })`; a mode of your own: `defineCamera` (`node x new camera`) | labs/game/main.ts, labs/playground/views.ts |\n| Touch controls                 | `defineTouchLayout`, `createEngine({ touchLayout })` (built, not yet tested on phones)   | labs/box/touch.ts                  |\n| A new entry of a kind          | `node x new <kind> labs/game/<dir>/<name>`: the module and its test from a template (`node x new` lists the kinds, tools/templates/README.md says where each goes; a new scene starts as a copy of play.ts instead) | tools/templates/                    |\n\nSaves, high scores and settings go in the page's store (`createStore`, never read by the sim), as labs/game/best.ts\nkeeps the best round.\n\n## 5. Rules that keep play reproducible\n\n- Sim-side code (labs/game/scenes/, labs/game/cast/) imports only engine/sim-api.ts; pages only engine/index.ts.\n  Never import three.js or Rapier directly: the public API re-exports what a game needs.\n- No clocks, timers, `Math.random`, `crypto`, DOM, storage, Web Audio or Node built-ins in sim-side code, and no\n  dynamic `import()`: lint refuses each, and its message names the fix. No network either: what the sim needs from\n  outside arrives as intents or settings. Write `Math.pow`, not `**`. Use the standard `Math`: the sim swaps in\n  portable ports while it steps, so the results match in Node and Chromium.\n- Time in steps; randomness from named streams; entities in `w.query`'s id order.\n- A sim-side module reads no files: a level's text lives in a string in the scene module.\n- Presentation never writes the sim: cameras, looks, particles, sound and the HUD change freely without changing a hash.\n- Units: metres, seconds, radians; +Y up, x east, z south; characters face +Z.\n\n**Assets.** Make them with code and data first: geometry, textures and materials as generators with parameters,\nsounds as voices and tracker notes, levels as text. A binary file (an image, a recorded sound, a model) needs the\nowner's approval: ask, and once they approve, list it in APPROVED-BINARIES.json at the game's root as\n`{ \"path\", \"reason\" }`, the reason their answer, who and when; `npm run check` fails a binary listed nowhere. Fonts\nare approved: anywhere in labs/game/, each with its license file beside it.\n\n## 6. Seeing it without a screen\n\nEvery command prints its verdict first, about 20 lines at most, and writes `out/<cmd>/<target>/report.json`;\n`node x help <cmd>` gives its manual.\n\n| Command                                                     | What it does                                                                     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `npm test`                                                  | The game's tests (Vitest), headless                                              |\n| `npm run check`                                             | Types, lint, format, `node x check` (the asset rules; the engine as pinned)       |\n| `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--steps n] [--seed s] [--set k=v] [--feel]` | Runs a scene in Node: its hash, events, entities; `--feel` measures the player |\n| `node x replay labs/game/replays [--browser sim] [--update] [--bisect]` | Checks replays' goldens; `--update` records them; `--bisect` finds where two runs part |\n| `node x shot labs/game [--cam code] [--set k=v] [--png]`    | Renders the page on WebGPU: coverage, visible objects, a text thumbnail; an image only with `--png` |\n| `node x eye labs/game/scenes/play.ts [--cam code] [--steps n]` | What a camera sees of the bodies, in Node with no GPU: a text picture and its legend (`__engine.eye()` in a page) |\n| `node x film labs/game --steps \"…\" [--compare ref]`         | Films scripted play as a contact sheet; `--compare` names the objects whose pixels changed |\n| `node x gallery <kind> [id] --page labs/game [--look id]`   | Films every prop, material, emitter, look, body or move as a labelled contact sheet; `#gallery/<kind>/<id>` opens one on any page |\n| `node x inspect --page labs/game`, then `node x eval \"<js>\"`, `node x dump`, `node x set <key> <value>` | A live headless page to question: the HUD through DOM queries, `__engine` |\n| `node x perf labs/game [--scene id]`                        | Performance as counters (ms per step and frame, draw calls), never frames per second |\n| `node x describe [kind [id]]`                               | The registries: every kind's fields and entries (materials, moves, looks, sounds…) |\n| `node x qa geo tex audio`                                   | Content QA by numbers: the engine's and the game's geometry, props, textures and sounds (labs/game/'s geometry, props, textures and sounds folders); a new or worse violation fails, and one the game accepts goes in its own `tests/baselines/qa-<family>-game.json` with the reason |\n| `node x new <kind> <id>`                                    | A new entry from its template, with its test                                     |\n| `node x audio sfx:<id>` · `node x anim find <words>`        | A sound rendered in Node, as numbers · the clip library searched                 |\n| `node x engine`                                             | Which engine the game is on, and whether its files are as shipped                |\n\nIn a page, `window.__engine` is the inspector, and a headless engine in tests (`createHeadless({ scene, seed,\nsettings })`) has the same members but the rendering ones: `__engine.help()` lists them (`step`, `hash`,\n`capture`, `restore`, `replay`, `input.press`, `input.record`, `set`, `state`, `shot`,\n`stats`…).\n\n## 7. Testing a game\n\n- The starter's test shows the four kinds every game keeps: its golden replay, a run that continues exactly after a\n  capture and a restore, the bot winning on 10 seeds, and a rule (the clock). Tests sit beside their module\n  (`play.test.ts`) and run with `npm test`.\n- Keep the bot as the game grows: teach it each new rule. \"The bot wins on 10 seeds\" keeps proving the game can be won,\n  and finds the seeds that softlock.\n- Check the UI through the DOM: the overlay's elements are `[data-ui]` (`node x inspect`, then `node x eval`).\n- When play changes on purpose, record the goldens again (`node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`) and\n  say why in the commit. When a hash changes by surprise, `node x replay <file> --browser sim --bisect` narrows it\n  down: to the step, entity and field where two runs part (Node and Chromium, or Node and Node again), or, when every\n  run agrees but they all left the goldens, to the checkpoints between which they did (then the code, data or a\n  dependency changed what the sim does). The usual causes: a `Math` function held in a variable, `**`, an iteration\n  whose order is not fixed, module state between runs, view settings or presentation reaching the sim, `async` code in\n  a step.\n\n## 8. Looking things up\n\n- **docs/API.md**: every public name with the first sentence of its doc comment, the public API first. The comment at\n  the link is the full manual: grep engine/ for the name.\n- **docs/ERRORS.md**: every error and advice code with its fix. Errors print as `[CODE] message`, and the fix says\n  what to do.\n- **The engine's file comments**: each module opens with what it is for, its rules and a runnable `@example`.\n- **`node x describe`** for data, **`node x help`** for commands, **`__engine.help()`** for the inspector.\n- **The engine's repository** (https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge) for its plan, its decisions and its tests.\n\n## 9. When the engine is in your way\n\n1. Work round it in the game: a component, a system, page code of your own.\n2. Write it in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md for an engine agent who has never seen your game: what you tried, what happened\n   (the command, the error code), what you expected, what you did instead. The owner collects these from every game.\n3. Never change the engine quietly: `npm run check` compares every engine file with engine-lock.json. When no\n   workaround exists, patch the file and list it under \"Local engine patches\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md, with why.\n4. The owner decides when a game moves to a newer engine (`node x engine --update`). It replaces nothing of the\n   game's own (it stops and names what is in the way); the engine's package.json entries win, and it names any it\n   drops or sets back. It says which root files it brought to the engine's templates, and which ones you edited\n   whose templates changed: compare those with tools/game/template/ and take what applies. After it: `npm ci`,\n   `npm run check`, `npm test`, and record the goldens again where play changed.\n\n## 10. The public API by area\n\n<!-- api-map:start: generated by `node x docs --write` from the area comments of engine/sim-api.ts, engine/anim/api.ts and engine/index.ts; edit those, not this section -->\nSim-side code imports these from engine/sim-api.ts; pages get them from engine/index.ts as well. Each name has the\nfirst sentence of its doc comment in docs/API.md, and its whole manual where it is declared.\n\n**Scenes**: defined as data with a setup and a step, run by x sim, x replay and createHeadless.\n`defineScene`, `defineDevAction`; types `Scene`, `SceneSpec`, `SceneAction`, `SceneSound`, `DevActionSpec`.\n\n**The world**: entities, components, systems, timers, events.\n`defineComponent`, `PHASES`; types `World`, `SimRng`, `WorldTimers`, `WorldEvents`, `Entity`, `With`, `SpawnSpec`, `ComponentOf`, `Phase`, `SystemFn`.\n\n**Registries and settings**: content as data, one schema for every setting.\n`defineKind`, `def`, `defineSettings`; types `Entry`, `Kind`, `KindSpec`, `Field`, `Schema`, `SimSettings`, `SettingValue`.\n\n**Intents**: what the sim reads from outside each step.\n`held`, `pressed`, `fromCamera`, `INTENT_KEYS`, `createInputBuffer`, `updateInputBuffer`, `released`, `buffered`, `consume`, `consumeAll`, `repeated`, `BUFFER_STEPS`, `REPEAT_DELAY`, `REPEAT_RATE`; types `Intents`, `IntentValue`, `CustomIntentKey`, `InputBuffer`.\n\n**Math**: three.js's math classes and the engine's helpers (MathUtils covers clamp, lerp, damp, smoothstep).\n`Vector3`, `Quaternion`, `Matrix4`, `Euler`, `Box3`, `Sphere`, `Ray`, `Plane`, `Color`, `MathUtils`, `approach`, `angDiff`, `lerpAng`, `approachAng`, `smoothDamp`, `ease`, `swingTwist`.\n\n**Randomness**: seeded streams (the world's are w.rng('<stream>')).\n`Rng`.\n\n**Events**: emitted with w.emit, heard with w.events.on.\ntypes `EventMap`, `Listener`, `TraceRecord`.\n\n**Levels**: a legend of glyphs, level text compiled to plain data (heights, solid grid, colliders, meshes, spawns).\n`compileLevel`, `validateLevel`, `defineGlyph`, `defineLevel`, `getLevel`, `floorHeight`, `topHeight`, `solidAt`, `tileAt`, `LEVEL_FORMAT`; types `CompileOptions`, `LevelSource`, `LevelProblem`, `Glyph`, `GlyphSpec`, `LevelEntry`, `CompiledLevel`, `LevelGlyph`, `BoxCollider`, `ColliderDescriptor`, `GroundDescriptor`, `MeshDescriptor`, `SpawnPoint`, `LightSpot`, `TileRect`, `Point3`.\n\n**Animation (WP 3.4)**: the humanoid skeleton (a root plus 23 joints) and its builds, poses with three.js quaternions, blending under bone masks, additive layers, forward kinematics, SVG contact sheets and the pose checks.\n`JOINTS`, `SOCKETS`, `MASKS`, `createSkeleton`, `boneMask`, `defineBuild`, `getBuild`, `Pose`, `blendPoses`, `makeAdditive`, `addPose`, `hashPose`, `forwardKinematics`, `createWorldPose`, `jointPosition`, `jointRotation`, `jointMatrix`, `jointToWorld`, `socketPosition`, `tipPosition`, `animSheet`, `animCheck`, `twoBone`, `twoBoneRot`, `LIMBS`, `aim`, `AIM_CHAIN`, `footPlant`, `createFootPlantState`, `FOOT_LOCK`, `FOOT_PLANT`, `FootPlant`, `queryGround`, `handTo`; types `JointName`, `SocketName`, `MaskName`, `Skeleton`, `BoneMask`, `Build`, `BuildSpec`, `JointRef`, `BlendOptions`, `WorldPose`, `Sheet`, `SheetFrame`, `SheetOptions`, `SheetView`, `AnimCheckSpec`, `AnimCheckReport`, `AnimProblem`, `RigidPair`, `PointName`, `TwoBoneOptions`, `LimbEnd`, `Limb`, `AimOptions`, `AimLink`, `FootPlantOptions`, `FootPlantState`, `FootLock`, `FootPlantLayerOptions`, `GroundQueries`, `GroundProbe`, `GroundHit`.\n\n**The procedural humanoid (WP 3.6)**: my-3d2dge's gait, idle, lean, arm swing, held poses and stances as effector targets written with B(forward, right, up), solved onto the skeleton; a fixed step and a seeded stream.\n`Humanoid`, `POSES`, `STANCES`, `WEAPONS`, `B`, `turnRight`; types `HumanoidOptions`, `HumanoidInput`, `HumanoidState`, `HumanoidWeights`, `BodyMotion`, `PoseName`, `StanceName`, `Weapon`, `Effectors`, `FRU`, `LimbSide`, `Sided`.\n\n**The character animator (WP 3.7)**: a body driven through an explicit layer stack (weights, base, override, solve, then layers in clips, additive, post, physics and secondary), told its body states each step (unknown words warn with the closest), with footsteps, sockets, a trail and detail levels; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n`Animator`, `BODY_STATES`, `checkBodyStates`, `TRAILS`, `SAMPLE_STATES`, `SLOTS`, `LAYER_SLOTS`, `LOD`, `easeWeight`, `HUMANOID_WEIGHTS`; types `AnimatorOptions`, `PoseOptions`, `AnimatorReport`, `AnimEvent`, `TrailSample`, `BodyStateName`, `AnimatorInput`, `TrailName`, `SampleState`, `SlotName`, `LayerSlot`, `AnimLayer`, `WeightRates`, `WeightInfo`.\n\n**Blob and floater rigs (WP 5.3)**: my-3d2dge's squash-and-stretch blob on a minimal skeleton (a volume-preserving squash spring, a smoothed look, wings, its moves as data: bite, leap, pounce, spit) and a floater that hovers over the floor, banks into turns and carries orbiting motes; step runs in the sim, pose in the view.\n`Blob`, `BLOB`, `BLOB_JOINTS`, `BLOB_LEAP`, `BLOB_MOVES`, `blobMove`, `blobMoveTimes`, `Floater`, `FLOATER`; types `BlobOptions`, `BlobInput`, `BlobState`, `BlobJoint`, `BlobPose`, `BlobLeap`, `BlobMove`, `BlobMoveName`, `BlobMoveFrame`, `FloaterOptions`, `FloaterInput`, `FloaterState`, `FloaterPose`.\n\n**The animation library (WP 8.4)**: readable key-pose clips (format 1 and 2: parse, print, check, the normalized lens, mirroring, the decode to 36 body points), loaded per set; baked to 30 fps rotation tracks on the skeleton, retargeted onto any build (rotation copy aligned at rest, the hip-height ratio, contact IK) or given to three.js as AnimationClip JSON; played by the clip layer in the animator's clip slot, loops at the ground speed, with shardfall's walk-run switch and strike time.\n`parseClip`, `parseClipSet`, `validateClip`, `poseError`, `poseMeanError`, `clipText`, `normalizedLens`, `mirrorClip`, `decodePose`, `samplePose`, `POSE_POINTS`, `POINT_INDEX`, `bakeClip`, `sampleBaked`, `travelAt`, `BAKE_FPS`, `retargetPose`, `clipJSON`, `hipRatio`, `standingHipHeight`, `restSkeleton`, `restPoints`, `restBuild`, `RETARGET`, `loadClipLibrary`, `clipPace`, `walkRate`, `runOverWalk`, `strikeTime`, `GROUND_SPEED`, `STRIKE`, `ClipLayer`, `CLIP_LAYER`; types `ClipSet`, `ClipSource`, `RestBody`, `ClipWarning`, `Clip`, `ClipKey`, `KeyPose`, `LimbPose`, `ClipEvent`, `ClipContact`, `BakedClip`, `RetargetOptions`, `AnimationClipJSON`, `ClipLibrary`, `ClipOrigin`, `ClipLayerOptions`, `ClipPlay`, `ClipSlot`, `ClipLayerState`, `ClipLayerEvent`, `ClipStepInput`.\n\n**Moves (WP 6.1)**: moves as data in metres (the kind move: my-3d2dge's 24, converted by Appendix C's table), timelines whose events carry leftover time, attacks and combos with hit windows, sweeps and root motion, moves played on a humanoid through its body state (the arm path), moves at a moment for previews, measured hit shapes, the cone helpers and a hit's knockback velocity.\n`defineMove`, `getMove`, `resolveMove`, `MOVE_PHASES`, `LEGACY_FIELDS`, `fromLegacy`, `Timeline`, `Attack`, `Combo`, `moveAt`, `measureMove`, `strikePart`, `strikeSegment`, `inArc`, `inHitShape`, `knockbackVelocity`; types `Move`, `MoveSpec`, `MoveInput`, `MovePhase`, `LegacyMove`, `TimelinePhase`, `TimelineMark`, `TimelineEvent`, `TimelineState`, `AttackState`, `ComboState`, `Sweep`, `HitShape`, `StrikeBody`, `StrikeAnimator`, `ArmState`.\n\n**Reactions and secondary motion (WP 6.2)**: a directional flinch, knockdown, death and the get-up chosen by how the body lies (face up or face down), stepped in the sim; verlet chains (hair, tails) with bone-capsule colliders, a floor probe and a rest shape, a cape of two chains, and the squash spring as a volume-preserving root scale, drawn by an animator layer in the view.\n`Reactions`, `REACTION`, `REACTIONS`, `lyingOf`, `Chain`, `CHAIN`, `Cape`, `Secondary`, `HAIR`, `CAPE`, `TAIL`, `SQUASH`, `squashScale`, `BODY_CAPSULES`, `bodyCapsules`; types `ReactionOptions`, `ReactionState`, `ReactionBody`, `ReactionName`, `Lying`, `ChainOptions`, `ChainTuning`, `BoneCapsule`, `CapeOptions`, `SecondaryOptions`.\n\n**Animation checks (WP 6.6)**: the state matrix (each body state held at each facing, from rest and back to rest) and animCheckBody, which checks a whole body over it: every pose, the same hashes twice, 1/60 s against 1/120 s; and the camera presets sheets of it are drawn from.\n`stateMatrix`, `STATE_MATRIX`, `animCheckBody`, `SHEET_CAMERAS`; types `MatrixState`, `MatrixRun`, `StateMatrixOptions`, `BodyCheckOptions`, `BodyCheckReport`, `AnimCheckName`.\n\n**Actions (WP 8.7)**: multi-beat sequences a body plays, beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and squashes with held contact frames, events, flags and cancel windows; the engine's set is data/anim/actions/, which loadActions defines.\n`Actions`, `defineAction`, `getAction`, `loadActions`, `actionCheck`, `ACTION`, `FLIP_AXES`, `EFFECTORS`, `CHANNELS`; types `ActionsOptions`, `ActionsState`, `Action`, `Beat`, `ActionSpec`.\n\n**Clip sets in the registry (WP 8.9)**: the kind clipset, one entry per set of readable clips, which a page or tool registers from data/anim/sets/ (defineClipSet), and the clip layer of an entity kept as plain data, loaded from it at every use (ClipLayerPool), as an actor's component clips holds it.\n`defineClipSet`, `clipSetFromFiles`, `getClipSet`, `clipSetId`, `clipSetIds`, `defineSetBuild`, `CLIPSET_FIELDS`, `CLIPS_FIELDS`, `emptyClipState`, `ClipLayerPool`; types `ClipSetEntry`, `ClipsData`.\n\n**The quadruped rig (WP 13.21)**: a dog on the planted gait for a crowd body (the rig `quadruped`), its state plain data, its pose the joints its body is drawn on, and its rig contract for the anim QA.\n`Quadruped`, `QUADRUPED`, `QUADRUPED_LEGS`, `QUADRUPED_RIG`; types `QuadrupedOptions`, `QuadrupedInput`, `QuadrupedState`, `QuadrupedPose`, `QuadrupedBone`.\n\n**Physics (WP 3.1)**: Rapier behind plain terms. A scene with `physics: true` has a physics world: bodies by id, raycasts, shape casts, overlaps and ground probes, collision groups by name, a level's bodies; presentation reads it through the read-only QueryView.\n`physicsOf`, `COLLISION_GROUPS`, `PHYSICS_SETTINGS`, `addLevelBodies`, `queryViewOf`; types `Physics`, `BodySpec`, `ColliderSpec`, `BodyState`, `BodyChange`, `BodyType`, `Shape`, `Vec3Like`, `QuatLike`, `Hit`, `Ground`, `QueryOptions`, `RayOptions`, `ShapeCastOptions`, `OverlapOptions`, `ProbeOptions`, `GroupName`, `QueryView`.\n\n**Characters (WP 3.2)**: actors moved by the character controller (Rapier's, moved across then down), with an action game's feel: acceleration and skid, coyote time, a jump buffer, a variable jump, a wall jump, a dash, pushing props; the feel meter measures how one handles. A body's reactions and an actor's secondary motion as components (WP 7.8), and its actions (`actOn`, WP 13.11).\n`createCharacter`, `CHARACTER_FIELDS`, `CHARACTER_STATE_FIELDS`, `spawnActor`, `removeActor`, `measureFeel`, `reactTo`, `stepReactions`, `REACTION_FIELDS`, `SECONDARY_FIELDS`, `actOn`, `ACTIONS_FIELDS`; types `Character`, `CharacterState`, `ActorSpec`, `ActorComponent`, `ActorComponents`, `FeelReport`, `FeelOptions`, `AnimatorComponent`, `SecondaryComponent`, `ReactionComponents`, `ActionComponents`.\n\n**Levels v2 (WP 4.2)**: the hall's glyphs (gallery =, stairs ^, dais o, brazier t), ramps and daises, hull and cylinder colliders, and the surface tag underfoot.\n`surfaceAt`; types `HullCollider`, `CylinderCollider`, `RampDescriptor`, `DaisDescriptor`, `Side`.\n\n**Navigation and spatial queries (WP 4.3)**: a level's walkable grid under a climb limit (the crowd's, or a hero's jump reach from measureFeel), A* paths smoothed by line of sight, cached flow fields with reach and a look-ahead direction, and a spatial hash on typed arrays for neighbours.\n`NavGrid`, `FlowField`, `NAV_GRID_FIELDS`, `NAV_DIRECTIONS`, `LOOK_AHEAD`, `NEAREST_RINGS`, `NAV_CLIMB`, `SpatialHash`, `SPATIAL_HASH_FIELDS`; types `NavGridOptions`, `FlowOptions`, `GroundPoint`, `SpatialHashOptions`.\n\n**Bodies and props (WP 4.1)**: props of one shape in the prototype's material, torque impulses and the knock; movers, kinematic bodies whose pose is a function of time, carrying riders; sensors and triggers; the lost-body rescue.\n`PROP_MATERIAL`, `addProp`, `addBox`, `addBall`, `addCapsule`, `addCylinder`, `applyTorqueImpulse`, `knock`, `MOVER_FIELDS`, `MOVER`, `moverPose`, `driveBody`, `driveMover`, `addSensor`, `sensorHits`, `TRIGGER_FIELDS`, `updateTrigger`, `rescueLost`; types `PropSpec`, `KnockOptions`, `Mover`, `SensorSpec`, `Trigger`, `RescueLevel`.\n\n**Props as data (WP 4.5)**: the registry kind `prop` (meshes by geometry and material id, a body, a light spot, where a level places it, the hit and use hooks), the engine's crate, barrel, brazier fire and pillar, the library's sconce, banner, chest, door, table, bench, statue and lantern (WP 10.3), and their placement.\n`defineProp`, `getProp`, `propCentre`, `levelProps`, `propBody`, `propPose`; types `Prop`, `PropDefinition`, `PropPart`, `PropTouch`, `PropKnock`, `PropPlacement`, `PropBody`, `PropPose`.\n\n**Props in a scene (WP 4.6)**: spawnProp stands a prop in a running scene (its body, and an entity with the component sceneProp whose pose is read back after each step, which the view draws as the prop); hitProp calls its hit hook with the means to knock it; removeProp.\n`spawnProp`, `removeProp`, `hitProp`, `SCENE_PROP_FIELDS`; types `ScenePropComponent`, `ScenePropComponents`.\n\n**Crowd bodies (WP 5.1)**: agents as dynamic capsules and balls driven by the velocities their AI wants (approach limits, only on their feet), knockback and launches, the corpse group and its timers, spawn and despawn queues.\n`CROWD_AGENT_FIELDS`, `CROWD_AGENT`, `CROWD_MATERIAL`, `CROWD_LIMITS`, `CROWD_GROUNDED`, `addAgent`, `removeAgent`, `setVelocity`, `knockback`, `launch`, `killAgent`, `updateCrowd`; types `CrowdAgent`, `AgentState`, `VelocityOptions`, `CrowdOptions`, `CrowdChanges`, `CrowdStepper`.\n\n**Steering kit (WP 5.2)**: the velocity a crowd agent wants (seek and arrive, flow-field following, approach rings, a flyer's hover, a jumper's hop), attack tokens that cap the attackers at once, and awareness by distance and sight.\n`seek`, `followFlow`, `approachRing`, `hover`, `airTime`, `hop`, `FOLLOW`, `APPROACH_RING`, `HOVER`, `HOP`, `ATTACK_TOKENS_FIELDS`, `ATTACK_TOKENS`, `takeToken`, `releaseToken`, `holdsToken`, `keepTokens`, `canSee`, `clearSight`, `SIGHT`; types `SteerPoint`, `FollowOptions`, `ApproachRingOptions`, `HoverOptions`, `AttackTokens`, `SightOptions`.\n\n**Crowd bodies (WP 5.7)**: a crowd member's look and rig (a humanoid in a build, a blob, a floater) in the component crowdBody, its rig stepped in the sim by stepCrowdBody; the stage draws them through a crowd with its level of detail.\n`CROWD_BODY_FIELDS`, `CROWD_BODY`, `stepCrowdBody`; types `CrowdBody`, `CrowdRig`, `CrowdBodyInput`.\n\n**Telegraphs (WP 6.4)**: an attack's warning shape on the floor as data (an arc, a ring, a line or a cone, in metres), and the geometry sim code tests it with as it goes off: a body inside it is hit, one just outside it a near miss.\n`TELEGRAPH_SHAPES`, `defineTelegraph`, `getTelegraph`, `telegraphGap`, `inTelegraph`, `nearMiss`, `telegraphFill`, `coneOf`; types `Telegraph`, `TelegraphSpec`, `TelegraphPlace`, `PointLike`.\n\n**Combat helpers (WP 6.3)**: hit queries against hurt capsules found through a spatial hash (cones with height windows, weapon sweeps), damage on health and crowd bodies (knockback as momentum, launches, death into the corpse group, the event damage), hit-stop handed to the frame clock's budget, and projectiles with swept tests.\n`PROJECTILE_FIELDS`, `PROJECTILE`, `stepProjectiles`, `capsuleCast`, `inCone`, `coneHits`, `sweepTouches`, `sweepHits`, `physicsWalls`, `HEALTH_FIELDS`, `HEALTH`, `damage`, `requestHitStop`; types `Projectile`, `ProjectileState`, `ProjectileStep`, `ProjectileHit`, `HurtCapsule`, `HurtSet`, `WallTest`, `WallContact`, `Health`, `DamageTarget`, `HitSpec`, `DamageEvent`, `DamageEvents`.\n\n**Melee (WP 13.19)**: Apex's punch, kicks and the door kick, a lunge to the target in reach, one hit a swing landing at its moment with its damage and hit-stop; the fan of rays that finds what a strike reaches.\n`MELEE`, `MELEE_MOVES`, `MELEE_FIELDS`, `addMelee`, `meleeTargets`, `fanHits`; types `MeleeState`, `MeleeReach`, `Fan`, `FanHit`.\n\n**Firearms (WP 13.20)**: the kind `weapon`, a player's weapons drawn and holstered, hip fire and the sights, hitscan shots with spread and a climbing recoil, reloads.\n`WEAPON_FIELDS`, `defineWeapon`, `getWeapon`, `recoilStep`, `spreadOf`, `WEAPONS_FIELDS`, `weaponsFor`, `addWeapons`; types `WeaponKind`, `WeaponSpec`, `WeaponsState`.\n\n**Ragdolls (WP 10.2)**: joints as data, a humanoid's body handed off to physics on a hard hit or a death, lying still and getting up the way it lies.\n`addJoint`, `removeJoint`, `hasJoint`, `jointBodies`, `RAGDOLL_SIM`, `RAGDOLL_FIELDS`, `addRagdolls`, `handOff`, `removeRagdollOf`, `standUp`; types `JointKind`, `JointSpec`, `RagdollState`, `RagdollHit`.\n\n**A town's daily round (WP 13.21)**: routes walked on a schedule, and stepping aside for someone in the way.\n`ROUTE_FIELDS`, `followRoute`, `ASIDE`, `stepAside`; types `RouteStop`, `RouteState`, `RouteLeg`.\n\n**A crowd body's ground (WP 13.21)**: the physics world's, for a gait's feet (a quadruped's).\n`crowdGround`.\n\n**Clips on actors (WP 8.9)**: the clip layer in an actor's animator as sim data, the component clips, stepped at the actor's ground speed and drawn over its body by the view; its events come as `clip`. spawnActor's `body` names the body it is drawn as (body:mannequin).\n`addClips`, `playClip`, `stopClip`, `seekClip`, `locomoteClips`, `clipLayerOf`; types `ClipsSpec`, `PuppetComponent`.\n\n**Movement profiles (WP 13.3)**: the registry kind `movement`, and the shooter model (Apex's) a profile gives a character.\n`defineMovement`, `getMovement`, `listMovements`, `MODEL_FIELDS`, `MOTION_FIELDS`; types `MovementProfile`, `MovementSpec`, `MovementModel`, `MovementModelSpec`, `MotionState`, `MotionFeel`.\n\n**The feel kit, sim-side (WP 13.4)**: the shooter model's live tuning, `movement.<group>.<field>` (a setting off its default replaces that number in every model character's model, from the next step), and an actor placed at rest (a scene's dev action: a feel course's start).\n`MOVEMENT_SETTINGS`, `movementOverrides`, `tuneCharacter`, `placeActor`; types `MovementOverride`.\n\n**Rides (WP 13.7)**: ziplines, ladders and launchers as world data (engine/world/traverse.ts), placed in a scene (engine/sim/rides.ts) and ridden by the shooter model (engine/physics/movement/ride.ts, ladder.ts): a zipline and the poles under its anchors, the redeploy balloon's line and its skydive, a ladder (or every ladder a level's ladder glyph `|` stands), a jump pad or a trampoline. Use is the button `interact`; the look's pitch picks a vertical zipline's way and a skydive's dive.\n`addZipline`, `addLadder`, `addLauncher`, `addLevelLadders`, `ridesOf`, `levelLadders`; types `ZiplineSpec`, `LadderSpec`, `LauncherSpec`, `Rides`, `RideCable`, `RideLadder`, `RideLauncher`.\n\n**Storeys (WP 13.13)**: format 2, levels written as storeys, read a storey at a time or in 3D: the highest surface at or under a point, its storey (by height, or by that surface) and its room.\n`LEVEL_FORMAT_2`, `storeyView`, `floorBelow`, `surfaceBelow`, `storeyAt`, `storeyBelow`, `roomAt`; types `StoreyDescriptor`, `RoomDescriptor`, `OpeningDescriptor`, `RoofDescriptor`.\n\n**Terrain (WP 10.5)**: the registry kind `terrain`, a seeded heightfield, its queries, its body and its navigation.\n`defineTerrain`, `getTerrain`, `makeTerrain`, `terrainProblems`, `TERRAIN_FIELDS`, `terrainHeight`, `terrainGradient`, `terrainNormal`, `terrainGrade`, `terrainSurface`, `scatterTerrain`, `addTerrainBody`, `terrainNavGrid`, `terrainNavSurface`; types `Terrain`, `TerrainSpec`, `TerrainEntry`, `TerrainHill`, `TerrainFlat`, `ScatterRule`, `TerrainPlace`, `TerrainNavOptions`, `NavSurface`, `NavSource`.\n\n**Sites (WP 13.15)**: levels standing on a terrain, each placed and turned in quarter turns, the terrain cut under them; the queries over all of it, the bodies, the ladders and the navigation over every storey and the terrain.\n`defineSite`, `getSite`, `makeSite`, `SITE_FIELDS`, `toWorld`, `toLocal`, `siteFloorBelow`, `siteSurfaceBelow`, `siteStructureAt`, `siteSolidAt`, `siteClearSight`, `siteRescueGrid`, `createSiteNav`, `addSiteBodies`, `addSiteLadders`; types `Site`, `SiteSpec`, `SiteStructure`, `StructureSpec`, `SiteRescueGrid`, `SiteNav`, `SiteNavOptions`.\n\n**The scene's props as navigation blocks (WP 13.21)**: what a site's walkers go round.\n`propObstacles`; types `NavBlock`.\n\n**Use (WP 13.16)**: the system `interact` (a player's use of the thing in reach and in view, with its prompt; carrying and throwing a light prop), the kinds `usable` and `strikable`, and the body-to-entity lookup.\n`addInteraction`, `defineUsable`, `defineStrikable`, `strikeEntity`, `USE_REACH`, `CARRY_MASS`, `THROW_SPEED`, `propOfBody`; types `UsableSpec`, `InteractorComponent`, `StrikeForce`, `SpawnPropOptions`, `PropHealthComponent`, `PropLightComponent`.\n\n**Doors (WP 13.16)**: hung on a hinge or sliding, opened away from the user, locked, blocked, burst open and broken.\n`addDoor`, `addLevelDoors`, `useDoor`, `strikeDoor`, `breakDoor`, `DOOR_SWING`; types `DoorSpec`, `DoorComponent`.\n\n**Windows and breakables (WP 13.16)**: props with health and their breaking, a level's window panes, a bolt's strike.\n`addLevelWindows`, `addBreakables`, `damageProp`, `breakProp`, `strikeBody`, `BREAK_SPEED`.\n\n**Switches (WP 13.16)**: a room's lights and the garage door, as sim state the view reads.\n`addSwitch`, `switchGroup`, `flipSwitch`; types `SwitchSpec`, `SwitchComponent`.\n\n**Strikes (WP 13.19)**: a strikable entry by entity (the move a melee plays on it: a door's kick), what takes one, and whose a body is (a prop's or an actor's, WP 13.20).\n`strikeEntry`, `takesStrike`, `entityOfBody`; types `StrikeEntry`.\n\n**Light and time (WP 13.23)**: the world's clock, and the light groups it switches at dusk and dawn.\n`setDaytime`, `daytimeOf`, `lightsAtDusk`, `litAt`, `DUSK`, `DAWN`; types `DaytimeComponent`, `DuskComponent`.\n\n**Things (WP 10.4)**: what can be hit, as a range's targets and a ring's dummies: the kind `thing` (its prop, hit points, rise, track and hooks), a thing's state (hits, down and up, its place on its track), and things in a scene (spawned as their prop, struck as hits, stood back up and driven along their tracks by the system `things`).\n`defineThing`, `getThing`, `thingState`, `hitThing`, `stepThing`, `thingAt`, `THING_FIELDS`, `THING_KIND_FIELDS`, `spawnThing`, `strikeThing`; types `Thing`, `ThingKind`, `ThingSpec`, `ThingTouch`, `ThingHit`, `ThingOutcome`, `ThingStanding`.\n\n**Projectile patterns (WP 10.4)**: a shot emitter's volleys (aimed, forward, ring, spiral, random) and timing (delay, interval, bursts and their pause, range), and the shots it looses over WP 6.3's projectiles.\n`SHOT_EMITTER`, `SHOT_EMITTER_FIELDS`, `PATTERNS`, `MOST_VOLLEYS`, `volley`, `stepVolleys`, `loose`, `direction`, `headingTo`; types `ShotEmitter`, `Pattern`, `VolleyAim`, `LooseOptions`.\n\n**Awareness (WP 10.4)**: beyond WP 5.2's sight, the noises an agent hears and its memory of where it last saw or heard.\n`AWARENESS`, `AWARENESS_FIELDS`, `AWARENESS_STATES`, `sense`, `hears`, `earshot`; types `Awareness`, `AwarenessState`, `Noise`, `SenseInput`, `Sensed`.\n\n**Springs and blends (WP 10.1)**: numbers, vectors and turns on exact damped springs with kicks; named weights eased up and down; the pose stack, angles averaged round the circle, a pitch and a yaw as a direction.\n`Spring1`, `Spring3`, `SpringQuat`, `StateWeights`, `layerPose`, `blendAngles`, `aimDirection`; types `SpringOptions`, `BlendRates`, `PoseLayer`.\n\n**Legs and bodies (WP 10.1)**: the digitigrade leg's reverse knee; sway chains and lagging parts; the planted gait for N legs in groups, its feet on raycast ground or gripping walls; path chains following their head.\n`digitigrade`, `SwayChain`, `LagPart`, `LegGait`, `PathChain`; types `DigitigradeLeg`, `DigitigradeFrame`, `DigitigradeSolve`, `SwayOptions`, `LagOptions`, `GaitLeg`, `GaitOptions`, `GaitFoot`, `GaitProbe`, `GaitBody`, `PathOptions`.\n\n**Faces and floating parts (WP 10.1)**: looking within bounds and blinking from a named stream; hovering, banking, leaning and folding; orbiters and detached parts; pose history played back late; extras on sockets; tip trails.\n`LookAt`, `Blink`, `Hover`, `Orbiters`, `DetachedPart`, `PoseHistory`, `socketMatrix`, `placeExtras`, `along`, `alongDirection`, `TipTrail`; types `LookAtOptions`, `BlinkOptions`, `HoveringOptions`, `OrbitOptions`, `DetachedOptions`, `HistoryOptions`, `Socket`, `SocketJoints`, `TrailOptions`, `TipSample`.\n\n**Reach and the rig contract (WP 10.1)**: an attack's reach measured off its animation; what a procedural rig names (its points, bones, feet and states) and the check it passes, as anim.check holds the humanoid.\n`measureReach`, `driveRig`, `rigCheck`, `rigClosed`; types `ReachPose`, `ReachOptions`, `Reach`, `ProcRig`, `ProcRigBody`, `RigRun`.","numLines":385,"startLine":1,"totalLines":479,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XeEXuuur9LATFxRMiw4For","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9LTC4ng4VWfZgfcXCZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QxMywXuNGuReRDfLfHu5XW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/README.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15038,"cache_read_input_tokens":32248,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15038},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"88fd7622-f127-438e-9010-26f4b82ec42d","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:12.057Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9LSsDHyZGh7akZTX7m","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QxMywXuNGuReRDfLfHu5XW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/README.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QxMywXuNGuReRDfLfHu5XW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n2\t\n3\tAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\n4\tplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\n5\tround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\n6\tscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\n7\tround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n8\t\n9\t**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n10\t\n11\tThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\n12\tdescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n13\t\n14\t- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n15\t- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n16\t\n17\tTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n18\t\n19\t> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n20\t> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n21\t> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## Modes\n26\t\n27\tA game declares one mode.\n28\t\n29\t| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n30\t|---|---|---|\n31\t| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n32\t| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n33\t| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n34\t\n35\t- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n36\t  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n37\t- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n38\t  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n39\t  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n40\t- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n41\t  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n42\t  world the host sends them.\n43\t\n44\tGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n45\t`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## A round\n50\t\n51\t1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n52\t   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n53\t   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n54\t   (*The card*).\n55\t2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n56\t3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n57\t4. The game reports how it goes.\n58\t5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n59\t   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n60\t   over the game, then removes it.\n61\t\n62\t### Tags\n63\t\n64\tIn the game's `<head>`:\n65\t\n66\t```html\n67\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n68\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n69\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n70\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n71\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n75\t  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n76\t  is in that range.\n77\t- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n78\t- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n79\t  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n80\t\n81\tOptional:\n82\t\n83\t- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n84\t  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n85\t- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n86\t  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n87\t\n88\t### The client\n89\t\n90\t[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\n91\tis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\n92\tafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n93\t\n94\t- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n95\t  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n96\t- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n97\t  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n98\t  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n99\t- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n100\t  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n101\t  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n102\t\n103\tThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\n104\tedited copy is not replaced.\n105\t\n106\t### Joining\n107\t\n108\t- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n109\t- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n110\t  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n111\t- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n112\t  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n113\t  the game runs as it does on its own.\n114\t- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n115\t  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n116\t- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n117\t  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n118\t  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n119\t\n120\t`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n121\t\n122\t| Field | |\n123\t|---|---|\n124\t| `me` | this copy's player id |\n125\t| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n126\t| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n127\t| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n128\t| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n129\t| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n130\t\n131\tNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\n132\tfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n133\t\n134\t### The card\n135\t\n136\tBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\n137\tnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\n138\tthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n139\t\n140\t```js\n141\tAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\tThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\n145\tscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\n146\ttitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\n147\tit deserves.\n148\t\n149\tWhile it is on, the card shows:\n150\t\n151\t- the game's name;\n152\t- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n153\t- how to win the round;\n154\t- the controls this player will use;\n155\t- the time left, from the `card` event.\n156\t\n157\tAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\n158\ton the card:\n159\t\n160\t- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n161\t  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n162\t- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n163\t  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n164\t\n165\tNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\n166\tchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\n167\tnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n168\t`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n169\t\n170\t`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n171\t\n172\t| Field | |\n173\t|---|---|\n174\t| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n175\t| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n176\t| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n177\t| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n178\t| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n179\t| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n180\t\n181\t### The start\n182\t\n183\t`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n184\t`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\n185\tgive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n186\t`start`.\n187\t\n188\t### Reporting\n189\t\n190\t- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n191\t  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n192\t  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n193\t- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n194\t  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n195\t  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n196\t- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n197\t  by the host only:\n198\t  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n199\t    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n200\t    placed last.\n201\t  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n202\t  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n203\t\n204\t### Time-up and the end\n205\t\n206\t- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n207\t  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n208\t  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n209\t  `finish()` by then is void.\n210\t- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n211\t  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n212\t  game, then removes it.\n213\t\n214\t| `end` reason | |\n215\t|---|---|\n216\t| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n217\t| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n218\t| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n219\t| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n220\t| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n221\t\n222\t### Leaving\n223\t\n224\t- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n225\t  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n226\t- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n227\t- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n228\t  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n229\t- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n230\t  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n231\t  that player leaving.\n232\t\n233\t### Scoring\n234\t\n235\t- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n236\t  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n237\t  progress.\n238\t- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n239\t  result instead of medals.\n240\t- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n241\t\n242\t`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\n243\tresults:\n244\t\n245\t- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n246\t  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n247\t- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n248\t  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n249\t\n250\tPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n251\t\n252\t---\n253\t\n254\t## Sending data between copies\n255\t\n256\t### Parallel\n257\t\n258\tThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n259\t\n260\t### Ghost\n261\t\n262\tEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\n263\tthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\n264\tonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\n265\tcharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n266\t\n267\t### Shared world\n268\t\n269\t- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n270\t  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n271\t- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n272\t  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n273\t  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n274\t- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n275\t  every copy.\n276\t\n277\t### `send()` and `message`\n278\t\n279\t- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n280\t  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n281\t- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n282\t  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n283\t  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n284\t- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n285\t- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n286\t  client drops the rest.\n287\t- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n288\t  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n289\t  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n290\t  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n291\t\n292\t---\n293\t\n294\t## Rules\n295\t\n296\tIn a session:\n297\t\n298\t- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n299\t  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n300\t- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n301\t  names. Names come from the hub.\n302\t- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n303\t  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n304\t  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n305\t- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n306\t  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n307\t  is held.\n308\t\n309\tEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\n310\tone, the game is not affected.\n311\t\n312\t---\n313\t\n314\t## Test it\n315\t\n316\tThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\n317\tand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\n318\tto the game:\n319\t\n320\t```bash\n321\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n322\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\n323\tcurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\n324\tpython3 -m http.server 8000\n325\t```\n326\t\n327\tThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\n328\tThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n329\t`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n330\t`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n331\t`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n332\t\n333\tThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n334\t`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n335\t`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n336\t\n337\t- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n338\t- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n339\t- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n340\t  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n341\t- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n342\t  player of its own;\n343\t- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n344\t  told the round ended;\n345\t- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n346\t\n347\t**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\n348\tcases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n349\t`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\n350\tgame is served from the harness's own origin.\n351\t\n352\tIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\n353\tchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\n354\truns there.\n355\t\n356\t---\n357\t\n358\t## Reference\n359\t\n360\t### Tags\n361\t\n362\t| Tag | |\n363\t|---|---|\n364\t| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n365\t| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n366\t| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n367\t| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n368\t| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n369\t| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n370\t| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n371\t\n372\t### API\n373\t\n374\t| | |\n375\t|---|---|\n376\t| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n377\t| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n378\t| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n379\t| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n380\t| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n381\t| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n382\t| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n383\t| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n384\t| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n385\t| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n386\t| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n387\t| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n388\t| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n389\t\n390\t`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\n391\tread them.\n392\t\n393\t| Event | Arguments |\n394\t|---|---|\n395\t| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n396\t| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n397\t| `message` | `data, from` |\n398\t| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n399\t| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n400\t| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n401\t\n402\tEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\n403\torder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\n404\treach the game through the client unchanged.\n405\t\n406\t### Versions\n407\t\n408\tThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\n409\tgames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\n410\tprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n411\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"ed28a5cc-daab-4743-b16f-1674d96cf6f3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:12.078Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/README.md","content":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","numLines":411,"startLine":1,"totalLines":411}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QxMywXuNGuReRDfLfHu5XW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9LTC4ng4VWfZgfcXCZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QSDNPnQDjUBSFWXSar6QxF","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15038,"cache_read_input_tokens":32248,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15038},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"28832fc1-bb0b-48ff-819b-dbf485c94b2b","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:12.759Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9LSsDHyZGh7akZTX7m","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QSDNPnQDjUBSFWXSar6QxF":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QSDNPnQDjUBSFWXSar6QxF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n2\t\n3\tThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\n4\tstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\n5\tpage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\n6\tis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n7\t[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\n8\tfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\n9\tand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\tYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\n14\tin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\n15\ttime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\n16\thow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\n17\taudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n18\t\n19\tA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n20\t`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\n21\tthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\n22\tagainst it:\n23\t\n24\t<protocol>\n25\t{{contract}}\n26\t</protocol>\n27\t\n28\tDo only this:\n29\t\n30\t1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n31\t   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n32\t2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n33\t   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n34\t   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n35\t   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n36\t   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n37\t   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n38\t3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n39\t   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n40\t   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n41\t   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n42\t   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n43\t4. Whenever the score changes, call\n44\t   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n45\t   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n46\t5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n47\t   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n48\t   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n49\t   still going.\n50\t6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n51\t7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n52\t   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n53\t   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n54\t   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n55\t8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n56\t\n57\tDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\n58\ttouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\n59\tthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\n60\tthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\n61\tlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\n62\tgame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\n63\teither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\n64\tdescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n65\t\n66\tIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\n67\tline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n68\t\n69\tOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\n70\tyou changed.\n71\t\n72\t{{notes}}\n73\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"088bed87-cb53-435f-af8c-f6cd614a9c61","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:24:12.783Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub/lib/hub/retrofit.md","content":"# Upgrading an existing game to parallel\n\nThe prompt `scripts/retrofit.mjs` gives a model for each game, after its tool\nstep has put the session client and the parallel metas at the top of the\npage. The model works in a copy of the game in a folder of its own: `{{file}}`\nis the game's page there, `{{contract}}` the protocol in\n[`README.md`](README.md) from *Modes* to *Rules*, word for word (the same text\nfor every game, so a batch reads it from the cache), `{{budget}}` the most lines a try may change,\nand `{{notes}}` what the last try's check said, if there was one.\n\n---\n\nYou are upgrading one existing browser game, `{{file}}`, so friends can play it\nin parallel mode in AI Hub sessions: each friend plays their own copy at the same\ntime, and the hub keeps the score. Change as little as possible. Never change\nhow the game plays or feels: movement, physics, timing, controls, menus, art,\naudio and difficulty stay exactly as they are.\n\nA tool has already put the session client (`session.js`, which defines\n`window.AIHub`) and the `hub:session` meta tags at the top of `<head>`. This is\nthe protocol; the game is upgraded to its parallel mode, and the hub checks it\nagainst it:\n\n<protocol>\n{{contract}}\n</protocol>\n\nDo only this:\n\n1. Find how the game is won or scored. Use a time (lower wins) if it has a\n   clear finish, otherwise its score (higher wins).\n2. Fill `hub:session-goal` (how to win, at most 60 characters) and\n   `hub:session-howto` (the controls, at most 80 characters), in plain words\n   for someone who has never seen the game. If a round of the game has a\n   fixed length, add `<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"…\">` with it.\n   If the game cannot run at all without WebGPU, also add\n   `<meta name=\"hub:webgpu\" content=\"required\">`.\n3. In session mode (`AIHub.active` is true): skip the title screen, attract\n   mode and menus; `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'parallel', seats: [1, 4] })`;\n   build the game; call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as it can start, with no \"press\n   start\" prompt; begin play only once `AIHub.time() >= 0`. If `connect()`\n   answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false.\n4. Whenever the score changes, call\n   `AIHub.progress({ score: <number>, label: <short text> })`, where a higher\n   score is further ahead and `label` is short text for the scoreboard.\n5. When the run ends (finished, died, out of lives), call\n   `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` once. In\n   `AIHub.on('time-up', …)`, report the current score at once if the run is\n   still going.\n6. In `AIHub.on('end', …)`, stop input and sound.\n7. If the game deals its pieces, cards, board or level layout from one\n   function using `Math.random`, make that function draw from\n   `AIHub.rng('deal')` in session mode, so every copy gets the same deal all\n   round. Leave every other random call alone.\n8. When `AIHub.active` is false, the game must behave exactly as before.\n\nDon't add features, refactor, reformat, rename or upgrade anything, and don't\ntouch code these steps don't need: change at most {{budget}} lines. Never edit\nthe session client between its `/*! aihub-session` banner and its last line,\nthe names of the meta tags, or a bundled library (three.js, Rapier and the\nlike). Saved progress is already hidden in a session (the client gives the\ngame an empty storage), and `Math.random` is already seeded: add no code for\neither. You are done when the game would pass the checks the protocol\ndescribes; you need not run them, the hub does.\n\nIf the game has no score and no finish, change nothing and reply with one\nline: `NOT PARALLEL: <why>`.\n\nOtherwise reply with: the score or time used, the end condition, and every line\nyou changed.\n\n{{notes}}\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QSDNPnQDjUBSFWXSar6QxF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
