{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj9RUKf624nT1kbsYqHv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LjUrPe5dPkM1cNSu4jfo8d","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 335,356p README.md; echo ......; sed -n 485,525p README.md; echo ......; sed -n 145,160p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ......; sed -n 220,232p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ......; sed -n 500,535p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo .....; grep -n \"dedicated\\|server\" docs/multiplayer-plan.md | head -30; echo; grep -n 'hub:players\\|hub:couch\\|hub:type' -r docs/*.md README.md | head -20","description":"Read the hub docs on couch co-op, tags and standalone play"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12620,"cache_read_input_tokens":173176,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12620},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"a86d55ac-ffcd-4644-94aa-c63026d87845","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:25:21.814Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj9RU5HfiScvM6FRLyVv","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LjUrPe5dPkM1cNSu4jfo8d":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub && sed -n 335,356p README.md; echo ......; sed -n 485,525p README.md; echo ......; sed -n 145,160p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ......; sed -n 220,232p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo ......; sed -n 500,535p docs/multiplayer-plan.md; echo .....; grep -n \"dedicated\\|server\" docs/multiplayer-plan.md | head -30; echo; grep -n 'hub:players\\|hub:couch\\|hub:type' -r docs/*.md README.md | head -20","description":"Read the hub docs on couch co-op, tags and standalone play"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01LjUrPe5dPkM1cNSu4jfo8d":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work/.scratch/hub"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjUrPe5dPkM1cNSu4jfo8d","type":"tool_result","content":"  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n......\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.\n\n### Bringing the library to sessions\n......\ngame.\n\n| Version 2 | Now |\n|---|---|\n| 0 Watch | Parallel and ghost: the live scoreboard (*Now*). Shared worlds: a copy with no player of its own, fed the same traffic (*Later*) |\n| 1 Parallel | Mode `parallel`, client 1.3.0 (*Now*) |\n| 2 Ghosts | Mode `ghost`: parallel, plus the other players shown while they are in the same area, never touching a world (`race` until Nov 2) |\n| 3 Together | Mode `shared-world`: the host's copy runs the world (`versus` and `co-op` until Nov 2) |\n| 4 Natural multiplayer | A shared world, in whatever form the game's own multiplayer takes |\n| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n\n**One mode per game.** A list of modes per game would mean a code path and a\nconformance run per mode in every game, for a choice the hub never needs to\nmake. The lobby needs no new picker either: the deal alternates between the\nparallel and ghost games and the shared worlds, so a dozen shared worlds are\nnot buried under hundreds of the others.\n......\n   controls lines, wire connect, ready, start, progress, result and end;\n   nothing else. A game with no score and no finish is declared unfit and\n   left alone.\n4. **Check, no model**: the page's static check (one pristine client before\n   every script, the metas, the calls) and a budget of 150 changed lines,\n   then `hub-check --retrofit`: two players with one leaving, no traffic,\n   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n   with the original (still draws, no new errors, client inactive,\n   `Math.random` untouched). Since Nov 2 no game is checked for controls or\n   a menu: every game keeps its own.\n5. **Retry and escalate**: a second try told what the check found, then the\n   next model up; past `--max-cost` dollars for one game (default $1) it\n   stops. A game none of them can wire is put back byte for byte, with a\n......\n  already talk gets most of the benefit.\n\n**Pinned** (parked until the owner wants it)\n\n- Remote couch co-op: friends far away playing a game made for one screen, on\n  the host's screen. It needs that screen streamed to them as video, with\n  their controllers sent back, so it waits for a decision to stream video.\n  Couch co-op in one room is the game's own and is unaffected.\n\n---\n\n## Part 7. cc-matrix\n\nNothing changes in cc-matrix. It is handed\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>, and the protocol's\nown one-paragraph ask (in *Start here*) is the line to add to a prompt.\nVersion 2's Appendix A is dropped: one source of truth, which the hub checks.\n\nThings in cc-matrix's own prompt that meet the protocol, for the owner when\nnext editing it:\n\n- **\"WebGPU, no fallback\"** (its tech stack) meets \"give it a WebGL 2\n  fallback\". Either drop \"no fallback\", or let such games declare\n  `hub:webgpu` `required`; the hub then deals them only to groups whose\n  browsers all have WebGPU.\n- **Attract mode after 10 s idle** on the title screen: in a session there is\n  no title screen, so it never starts there. No conflict.\n- **Testing \"efficient\"**: the agent can still run the harness from the\n  protocol's *Test it*; the hub checks every game at import either way.\n- **Up to four players locally, controller first**: welcome. Couch co-op in\n  one room is the game's own; remote couch co-op is pinned.\n\nIf cc-matrix builds are imported, the hub's importer (`import-matrix-runs.mjs`,\nwritten for gpt-matrix's layout) needs to learn cc-matrix's `runs/<id>/work/`\nlayout: an ai-hub change, made when the first cc-matrix builds come in.\n\n.....\n44:  Dedicated servers: the games are made ready now, with one rule that the\n45:  check tests; the server itself is built when it is wanted.\n132:| 4 | The host is the server, built so a dedicated server can take over later | **Kept: the games are made ready now, the server is built when wanted** | The host's copy runs every shared world today. What multiplies across thousands of games is each game's own part, so that goes in now: a shared world must run when its host is a copy with no player of its own (`AIHub.server`), and `hub-check` plays one such round for every versus and co-op game. Two old rules stay as guidance (the world in plain data apart from drawing; nothing read back from the GPU). Built once, and likely to change before it is needed, so built later: the server program, where it runs, how it joins a session, and the lobby's switch. |\n135:The owner decided both on Oct 29: no video, and servers prepared in the\n414:- Dedicated servers, the part in the games: a shared world must run when its\n416:  round for every versus and co-op game; the round takes a server seat;\n418:  they play as before and get it when servers come.\n464:- **Dedicated servers**, when wanted: the server program (a browser with no\n468:  the lobby's switch. A small server hosts a few sessions at once for about\n542:| 1 | Decisions 3 and 4 | Decided Oct 29: state, not video, and no video anywhere (remote couch pinned); dedicated servers prepared in the games now, built when wanted |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"8930b333-3dae-4464-9c3d-8a6eb4f81bdb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:25:21.849Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows