n your looks.''','''Draw it the way the engine's own clean playground look does: `resolution: 'full'`, `textures: 'smooth'`, and the `aa` filter with `scope: 'all'`, in your looks. Do **not** use the chunky default `pixels` resolution: thin surgical lines must read at 10 feet. `labs/box/looks/` has worked looks to copy from.''')\nrep('''10. You share this machine.''','''10. The guide cites `labs/playground/`, but a game gets only `labs/game/` and `labs/box/`. To read playground code you would need the engine's repository; this plan does not.\n11. You share this machine.''')\nrep('every 5 s a state hash','about once a second a state hash')\nrep('**Boot order, tested against the real harness. Each line here fixes a failure seen there.**','**Boot order, tested against the real harness. The comments mark failures seen there.**')\nrep('Keep the card up until `engine.ready`; on real GPUs that is about a second.','The card is HTML over the 3D shot, so it can show before the first frame. If the countdown ends before `engine.ready`, keep a plain cover up until it is; ticks still run behind it. On real GPUs this should take moments.')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint(s.count('labs/playground'), [m for m in ['exit-interview','GameTitle','.scratch'] if m in s])\nimport re\nrefs=set(re.findall(r'§(\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)',s))\nheads=set(re.findall(r'^##+ (\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?)[. ]',s,flags=re.M))\nprint('bad refs:',sorted(r for r in refs if r not in heads))\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',len(s.encode()),'bytes')\nEOF\nsed -n '/^## 17/,/^### 17.2/p' PLAN.md | cut -c1-200 | head -34","description":"Apply final review fixes and validate the plan's cross-references"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0162k1DfWhW1kZXjTchE5mYq","type":"tool_result","content":"1 []\nbad refs: []\n11544 words 70508 bytes\n## 17. Engine and architecture\n\n### 17.1 Setup, and traps already hit on this machine\n\nThis plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.\n\n```sh\n# in the empty dev folder\nexport TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/g.XXXXXX)        # trap 1\nexport CHROMIUM_PATH=$(ls -d ~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome | head -1)   # trap 2\nsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" . --name <your-slug>\nnpm ci\n```\n\n1. **A long `TMPDIR` breaks tools silently.** The run's `TMPDIR` is too long for a Unix socket path. Chromium dies with `Socket path too long` and `node x shot` then hung for over 4 minutes; `npx tsx`\n2. **Do not run `bash scripts/setup.sh`** even though the output and `AGENTS.md` suggest it: it would download Node and Chrome for Testing. `npm ci` plus `CHROMIUM_PATH` is enough. The path above is C\n3. **Don't use `node x shot --cam3`:** the flag is `--cam` (`node x help shot`).\n4. A running page **cannot switch scenes**; the guide's page reload is not allowed in a hub session. Use §17.2. **Every query key is read as a setting**: an unknown one stops the engine, which is why \n5. The engine's `modal` stops intents but not the sim: pause with `inspector.pause()` too.\n6. The engine's gamepad device gives one active pad, and another pad used can take over. For gameplay, read pads yourself (§17.4). Use engine input for menus, keyboard and mouse.\n7. Every source file under `labs/` needs a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines (hard cap 600), `.mjs` tools included. Run the formatter before `npm run check`.\n8. Game code may not import three.js (`npm run check` refuses it). The recipes below stay legal.\n9. Golden replays break whenever play changes on purpose: `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`.\n10. The guide cites `labs/playground/`, but a game gets only `labs/game/` and `labs/box/`. To read playground code you would need the engine's repository; this plan does not.\n11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder, keep one or two browsers open, and stop only processes you started, by PID. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves.\n\nMeasured on the starter: `npm ci` 6 s, `npm run check` 25 s, `npm test` 9 s, `node x shot` 12 s, a one-file build 8 s.\n\nTested and working, so build on these: the live mesh of §17.3; one-file build and boot from `file://`; frames still drawing while paused; `step(n, intents)` with custom keys; restart with new settings\n\n### 17.2 One scene, many runs","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"1cbc456f-f70c-4748-99c3-810c04d5eac7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:08:29.393Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1 []\nbad refs: []\n11544 words 70508 bytes\n## 17. Engine and architecture\n\n### 17.1 Setup, and traps already hit on this machine\n\nThis plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.\n\n```sh\n# in the empty dev folder\nexport TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/g.XXXXXX)        # trap 1\nexport CHROMIUM_PATH=$(ls -d ~/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome | head -1)   # trap 2\nsrc=$(mktemp -d) && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \"$src\" && node \"$src/tools/game/new-game.mjs\" . --name <your-slug>\nnpm ci\n```\n\n1. **A long `TMPDIR` breaks tools silently.** The run's `TMPDIR` is too long for a Unix socket path. Chromium dies with `Socket path too long` and `node x shot` then hung for over 4 minutes; `npx tsx`\n2. **Do not run `bash scripts/setup.sh`** even though the output and `AGENTS.md` suggest it: it would download Node and Chrome for Testing. `npm ci` plus `CHROMIUM_PATH` is enough. The path above is C\n3. **Don't use `node x shot --cam3`:** the flag is `--cam` (`node x help shot`).\n4. A running page **cannot switch scenes**; the guide's page reload is not allowed in a hub session. Use §17.2. **Every query key is read as a setting**: an unknown one stops the engine, which is why \n5. The engine's `modal` stops intents but not the sim: pause with `inspector.pause()` too.\n6. The engine's gamepad device gives one active pad, and another pad used can take over. For gameplay, read pads yourself (§17.4). Use engine input for menus, keyboard and mouse.\n7. Every source file under `labs/` needs a `@file` comment and stays under 400 lines (hard cap 600), `.mjs` tools included. Run the formatter before `npm run check`.\n8. Game code may not import three.js (`npm run check` refuses it). The recipes below stay legal.\n9. Golden replays break whenever play changes on purpose: `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim --update`.\n10. The guide cites `labs/playground/`, but a game gets only `labs/game/` and `labs/box/`. To read playground code you would need the engine's repository; this plan does not.\n11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder, keep one or two browsers open, and stop only processes you started, by PID. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves.\n\nMeasured on the starter: `npm ci` 6 s, `npm run check` 25 s, `npm test` 9 s, `node x shot` 12 s, a one-file build 8 s.\n\nTested and working, so build on these: the live mesh of §17.3; one-file build and boot from `file://`; frames still drawing while paused; `step(n, intents)` with custom keys; restart with new settings\n\n### 17.2 One scene, many runs","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0162k1DfWhW1kZXjTchE5mYq","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
