{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjDDgDQhNbuvBZYxMZvL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Verify now passes under the long TMPDIR, printing only failures and exiting 1. I also found that my no-WebGPU message could never show in Firefox, since the build crashes at import, so that check moves into a classic inline script as I update the plan.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3285,"cache_read_input_tokens":548296,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3285},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"80e15d6c-4260-4640-8183-7a81188863e8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:32.753Z","request_id":"req_011CgjDDfwYNYaDyh4atBkUi","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":1,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjDDgDQhNbuvBZYxMZvL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J6v3SeQBVVX5S6EeUBi7uB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nverify=open('.scratch/spike/labs/game/tools/verify.mjs',encoding='utf-8').read().rstrip()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# --- §1 scope\nrep('''**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**''','''**Scope.** A feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice. It turns what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, into mechanics, pacing and UX. It is long enough to show every feature, so a player understands how it plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do (§4's refusals, §10's Handbook). It holds all the infrastructure needed to build out the rest with normal designer tools (§17.5).\n\n**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**''')\n\n# --- §4 refusals\nrep('''**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.''','''**Never silent: the game says what you cannot do.** Using a tool on something it cannot affect gives a dull thud, a \"no\" icon at the cursor and one line from Odie naming the right tool (\"Laser won't touch that, Doctor. It likes the warmth.\"), with no penalty. A table in `labs/game/data/tools.ts` gives every tool and target kind either an effect or a refusal line, and it drives this feedback, the bot and the Handbook; a test fails if any pair is missing.\n\n**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.''')\n\n# --- §10 screens\nrep('''- **Boot/Loading:** dark, lighthouse beam, status text; the engine error text stays visible if startup fails. With no WebGPU (the engine has no fallback), show a large plain message saying the game needs a browser with WebGPU, with `AIHub.fail` in a session.''','''- **Boot/Loading:** dark, lighthouse beam, status text; the engine's error text stays visible if startup fails for any other reason.\n- **No WebGPU:** the engine has no fallback, and this cannot be left to game code. Tested: in Firefox here the bundle crashes at import (`APP_START: … \"VERTEX\", Js is undefined`), before any of your module runs; WebKit shows the engine's technical `GFX_NO_WEBGPU` text. So a **classic inline script** in the head, right after the hub client, checks `navigator.gpu`. If it is missing, it shows a large plain message saying the game needs a browser with WebGPU, adds \"Controller or keyboard required\" on a touch-only device, calls `AIHub.fail(…)` in a session, and hides the engine's text. Do the same if `__engine.errors` later holds `GFX_NO_WEBGPU` (no adapter).''')\nrep('''- **Pause:** Resume, Retry phase, Retry case, Settings, Controls, **Dev Mode**, Quit to Board.''','''- **Pause:** Resume, Retry phase, Retry case, Handbook, Settings, Controls, **Dev Mode**, Quit to Board.\n- **Handbook** (from Pause and the Board): big paged cards showing what you can and cannot do. One per instrument (what it does, what it cannot do, its controls in the current glyphs), the Gift, flaps, sites and the Code, and one per strain once met. Built from the data, so it cannot go stale.''')\n\n# --- §17.1 workspace, machine\nrep('''This plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.''','''Use the engine's **full workspace, not the lean** single page: you want its headless tests, replays and `node x` tools. The rest of the stack is what it ships: TypeScript and Vite, with no new npm dependencies. This plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.''')\nrep('''11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder, keep one or two browsers open, and stop only processes you started, by PID. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves.''','''11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder and keep one or two browsers open. Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`, and never kill whatever holds a port. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools; start a server only for the hub harness.\n12. **The browsers are already downloaded: never run `playwright install`.** The engine's own `@playwright/test` (1.64.1) drives the downloaded Chromium through `executablePath` (tested). TASK.md's `npm install playwright@1.63.0` would change the game's pinned dependencies, so for Firefox and WebKit use the system Python's Playwright instead (tested): `from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright`.\n13. Runs have no display, so all of this is headless; a headed browser would need `xvfb-run`. WebGPU needs a secure page: `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`.\n14. `earlyoom` is on. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying, and run the four-copy harness alone.\n15. The system Python has only Playwright. For anything else: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` if it drives a browser.''')\n\n# --- §18 note\nrep('''- `inlineBuild`'s note names the playground: replace that comment with your own.''','''- `inlineBuild`'s note names the playground: `.replace(ONE_FILE_NOTE, '<!-- your note -->')`, also exported from `tools/lib/oneFile.ts` (tested).''')\n\n# --- §19 verify\nrep('''**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`, added to `package.json`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.''','''**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\n\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\n\n```js\n'''+verify+'''\n```\n\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.''')\nrep('''- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.''','''- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.''')\nrep('''- Touch profile: exact message, settings button works, no touch stick, attract runs.\n- No WebGPU: Firefox and WebKit here have WebGL only, so open the file there and expect the plain message, with no crash.''','''- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.''')\nrep('''- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an analyser hears music and a beep; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.''','''- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.''')\n\n# --- §21 fields, catalogue, feedback\nrep('''One screen each: Source Information (every field of TASK.md's list), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits. Use \"unknown\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`.''','''One screen each: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage, with your TASK.md's values for Date, Environment and Model), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback (every question TASK.md asks, one card each), credits. Use \"unknown\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`.''')\nrep('''Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. `name` is your title as on the title screen.''','''Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\"; 3 to 6 tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. Write your own `tagline` (a few words) and `description` (one or two sentences for the catalogue), in plain text. `name` is your title as on the title screen. Answer the feedback sections from your issue log (§19) and `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`.''')\n\n# --- coverage table\nrep('''| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |''','''| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |\n| Player sees what they can and cannot do | §1, §4 refusals, §10 Handbook |''')\nrep('''| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §21 |''','''| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §19 issue log, §21 |''')\nrep('''| Testing: headless, one command, playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |''','''| Testing: headless, one command (tested skeleton), playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |''')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',len(s.encode()),'bytes')\nEOF\ncd .scratch && sed -i \"s/'can_cannot':  (72, 'what they can and cannot do', \\[r'\\\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\\\*Handbook', r'cannot do'\\]),/'can_cannot':  (72, 'what they can and cannot do', [r'\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Handbook\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*', r'what you cannot do', r'refusal line']),/\" audit.py; grep -n \"can_cannot\" audit.py | cut -c1-130; python3 audit.py | grep -v \"^ok \"","description":"Patch the plan for every gap the audit found, then rerun the audit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3285,"cache_read_input_tokens":548296,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3285},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41ecbf1a-95aa-45b3-a227-3833f8bfa7e2","uuid":"11b56cf7-974f-4882-ba3e-944b2caf3204","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:16:04.967Z","request_id":"req_011CgjDDfwYNYaDyh4atBkUi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01J6v3SeQBVVX5S6EeUBi7uB":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211917__x554572/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\nverify=open('.scratch/spike/labs/game/tools/verify.mjs',encoding='utf-8').read().rstrip()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert a in s, a[:90]\n    s=s.replace(a,b,1)\n\n# --- §1 scope\nrep('''**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**''','''**Scope.** A feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice. It turns what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, into mechanics, pacing and UX. It is long enough to show every feature, so a player understands how it plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do (§4's refusals, §10's Handbook). It holds all the infrastructure needed to build out the rest with normal designer tools (§17.5).\n\n**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**''')\n\n# --- §4 refusals\nrep('''**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.''','''**Never silent: the game says what you cannot do.** Using a tool on something it cannot affect gives a dull thud, a \"no\" icon at the cursor and one line from Odie naming the right tool (\"Laser won't touch that, Doctor. It likes the warmth.\"), with no penalty. A table in `labs/game/data/tools.ts` gives every tool and target kind either an effect or a refusal line, and it drives this feedback, the bot and the Handbook; a test fails if any pair is missing.\n\n**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.''')\n\n# --- §10 screens\nrep('''- **Boot/Loading:** dark, lighthouse beam, status text; the engine error text stays visible if startup fails. With no WebGPU (the engine has no fallback), show a large plain message saying the game needs a browser with WebGPU, with `AIHub.fail` in a session.''','''- **Boot/Loading:** dark, lighthouse beam, status text; the engine's error text stays visible if startup fails for any other reason.\n- **No WebGPU:** the engine has no fallback, and this cannot be left to game code. Tested: in Firefox here the bundle crashes at import (`APP_START: … \"VERTEX\", Js is undefined`), before any of your module runs; WebKit shows the engine's technical `GFX_NO_WEBGPU` text. So a **classic inline script** in the head, right after the hub client, checks `navigator.gpu`. If it is missing, it shows a large plain message saying the game needs a browser with WebGPU, adds \"Controller or keyboard required\" on a touch-only device, calls `AIHub.fail(…)` in a session, and hides the engine's text. Do the same if `__engine.errors` later holds `GFX_NO_WEBGPU` (no adapter).''')\nrep('''- **Pause:** Resume, Retry phase, Retry case, Settings, Controls, **Dev Mode**, Quit to Board.''','''- **Pause:** Resume, Retry phase, Retry case, Handbook, Settings, Controls, **Dev Mode**, Quit to Board.\n- **Handbook** (from Pause and the Board): big paged cards showing what you can and cannot do. One per instrument (what it does, what it cannot do, its controls in the current glyphs), the Gift, flaps, sites and the Code, and one per strain once met. Built from the data, so it cannot go stale.''')\n\n# --- §17.1 workspace, machine\nrep('''This plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.''','''Use the engine's **full workspace, not the lean** single page: you want its headless tests, replays and `node x` tools. The rest of the stack is what it ships: TypeScript and Vite, with no new npm dependencies. This plan was tested against `my-3dge@e7a1243`. `git clone --depth 1` may give you something newer: `docs/API.md` is the truth.''')\nrep('''11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder, keep one or two browsers open, and stop only processes you started, by PID. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves.''','''11. You share this machine. Stay in your own folder and keep one or two browsers open. Stop only processes you started, by PID: never `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`, and never kill whatever holds a port. The `x` commands pick a free port by themselves. Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools; start a server only for the hub harness.\n12. **The browsers are already downloaded: never run `playwright install`.** The engine's own `@playwright/test` (1.64.1) drives the downloaded Chromium through `executablePath` (tested). TASK.md's `npm install playwright@1.63.0` would change the game's pinned dependencies, so for Firefox and WebKit use the system Python's Playwright instead (tested): `from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright`.\n13. Runs have no display, so all of this is headless; a headed browser would need `xvfb-run`. WebGPU needs a secure page: `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`.\n14. `earlyoom` is on. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game: run fewer browsers rather than retrying, and run the four-copy harness alone.\n15. The system Python has only Playwright. For anything else: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` if it drives a browser.''')\n\n# --- §18 note\nrep('''- `inlineBuild`'s note names the playground: replace that comment with your own.''','''- `inlineBuild`'s note names the playground: `.replace(ONE_FILE_NOTE, '<!-- your note -->')`, also exported from `tools/lib/oneFile.ts` (tested).''')\n\n# --- §19 verify\nrep('''**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`, added to `package.json`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.''','''**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\n\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\n\n```js\n'''+verify+'''\n```\n\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.''')\nrep('''- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.''','''- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.''')\nrep('''- Touch profile: exact message, settings button works, no touch stick, attract runs.\n- No WebGPU: Firefox and WebKit here have WebGL only, so open the file there and expect the plain message, with no crash.''','''- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.''')\nrep('''- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an analyser hears music and a beep; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.''','''- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.''')\n\n# --- §21 fields, catalogue, feedback\nrep('''One screen each: Source Information (every field of TASK.md's list), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback, credits. Use \"unknown\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`.''','''One screen each: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage, with your TASK.md's values for Date, Environment and Model), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback (every question TASK.md asks, one card each), credits. Use \"unknown\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`.''')\nrep('''Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. `name` is your title as on the title screen.''','''Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\"; 3 to 6 tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. Write your own `tagline` (a few words) and `description` (one or two sentences for the catalogue), in plain text. `name` is your title as on the title screen. Answer the feedback sections from your issue log (§19) and `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`.''')\n\n# --- coverage table\nrep('''| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |''','''| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |\n| Player sees what they can and cannot do | §1, §4 refusals, §10 Handbook |''')\nrep('''| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §21 |''','''| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §19 issue log, §21 |''')\nrep('''| Testing: headless, one command, playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |''','''| Testing: headless, one command (tested skeleton), playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |''')\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint(len(s.split()),'words',len(s.encode()),'bytes')\nEOF\ncd .scratch && sed -i \"s/'can_cannot':  (72, 'what they can and cannot do', \\[r'\\\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\\\*Handbook', r'cannot do'\\]),/'can_cannot':  (72, 'what they can and cannot do', [r'\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Handbook\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*', r'what you cannot do', r'refusal line']),/\" audit.py; grep -n \"can_cannot\" audit.py | cut -c1-130; python3 audit.py | grep -v \"^ok \"","description":"Patch the plan for every gap the audit found, then rerun the audit"}}}
